2800:
** κλάσ-ις** [ᾰ], εως, ἡ,
(κλάω A) **breaking, fracture**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 43e}}; ἡ κ. τῶν ἀμπέλων **breaking off the shoots and tendrils** of vines, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.14.4 (_plural_), compare 3.7.5, +others; ἡ κ. τοῦ ἄρτου NT.Luke.24.35.}}
**2** **bending** of the knee joint, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 882b33}}; κ. ὄψεων **refraction**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteorologicorum libros commentaria - pars” 143.9; τὸ σαμεῖον περὶ ὃ ἁ κ. 4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus cited in 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 785.25.}}
**2.b** κλάσιν λαβεῖν **to be deflected**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.9.8}}; ὅταν κλάσιν ποιῇ καὶ γωνίαν, of a bandage. Erot. see at {σκέπαρνος}; of the labyrinth of the ear, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 8.6.}}
**II** **modulation** of the voice, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.276, 2.266.}}

2801:
** κλάσ-μα**, ατος, τό,
**fragment, morsel,** {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.1425.347,368, LXX.1Ki.30.12, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.13, NT.Mark.6.43, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 19, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.304 (Phanias Epigrammaticus), 11.153 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus); μελάθρων κλάσματα “Inscription Délos” 400.44 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II** **lesion, rupture**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 110.31.}}

2806:
** κλάω** [ᾰ],
**A** _imperfect_ ἔκλων (κατ-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.227, (ἀν-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76}}: _future_ κλάσω [ᾰ] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 10.11.3, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 11.1}}: _aorist 1_ ἔκλᾰσα, _Epic dialect_ κλάσε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.128, κατά-κλασσε 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.147}}:—_middle_, _poetry_ _aorist_ κλάσσατο {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.124 (4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus Laert.)}}:—_passive_, _future_ κλασθήσομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 373a5}}: _aorist_ ἐκλάσθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.584}}: _perfect_ κέκλασμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 76b9, etc.:}}_aorist 2_ _participle_ κλάς (as if from κλῆμι) {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 153}}:—**break, break off**, ἐξ ὕλης πτόρθον κλάσε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.128; ἐκλάσθη δὲ δόναξ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.584}}; **break off** the luxuriant shoots of the vine, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.15.1 (_passive_), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.134, 3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 3.29, etc.; κ. ἄρτον NT.1Cor.10.16, compare 11.24 (_passive_).}}
**A.2** Geometry texts, **deflect, inflect**, usually of drawing a straight line 'broken back' at a line or surface, κλάσαι εὐθεῖαν τὴν ΑΓΒ ἐν λόγῳ τῷ δοθέντι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 904.17}}; ἀπὸ δύο σημείων τῶν B, E κλάσαι τὴν ΒΝΞΕ {{Refs|[prev. author] 122.3}}:—more frequently in _passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 1.c}}.; ἡ κεκλασμένη (i.e. γραμμή) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 228b24; αἱ κλώμεναι εὐθεῖαι 3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 2.52}}; ἐὰν ἀπὸ τῶν σημείων κλασθῶσιν{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 3.52; κεκλάσθω 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 3.20, +others}}; of visual rays, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 377b22, “Pr.” 912b29}}; of arteries, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 9.84: generally, καμπαῖς κεκλασμένας ὑποπορεύσεις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.968b; κεκλ. στολίδες [prev. work]64a; τὰ κλώμενα τῶν ῥευμάτων}} their **broken** courses, {{Refs|[prev. work]747d.}}
**A.3** _metaphorically_, **break, weaken, frustrate**, τὴν ἐλπίδα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 3.7.13, compare “epigram Gr.” 348 (from Cios): in}}_perfect_ _participle_ _passive_, κεκλασμένη φωνή **weak, effeminate** voice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.80, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 813a35}} (also of the Siren's song, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 108.28, compare κ. ἀοιδή 242.10)}}; τὰ κεκλ. τῶν ὀμμάτων **enfeebled** eyes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 808a9}}; κεκλ. μέλη **varied by modulation**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1138c}}; ῥυθμὸς κεκλ. **broken** rhythm, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 41.1}}; τὸ κεκλ. καὶ παντοδαπόν (i.e. τῆς λέξεως) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.198S.}}
**A.3.b** of emotion, ἐκλάσθην πρὸς ἔλεον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Vita” 43.}}

2807:
** κλείς**, ἡ,
_genitive_ κλειδός; _Attic dialectaccusative_ κλεῖν, see.below 1.3, 111, later κλεῖδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.306 (from Aristo), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 9: _plural_ κλεῖδες, κλεῖδας, _contraction_ κλεῖς, see below 111, _dative_ κλεισίν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 371b}}:—_Ionic dialect_ **κληΐς** [ῑ], κληῗδος, κληῗδα, etc. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses only the _Ionic dialect_ form)}}:—_Doric dialect_ **κλᾱΐς**, κλαΐδος [ῐ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 23, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.39; but _accusative_ _plural_ κλᾱῗδας [prev. work] 8.4}}; _accusative_ κλαῖδα or κλᾷδα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Cererem” 45}}; compare κλᾴξ:—_Aeolic dialect_ **κλᾶϊς** (κλαῖς codex)· μοχλός, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; κλάϊς _accusative_ κλάϊν {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Etymologicum Gudianum” cited in Schaefer “Greg.Cor.” p.584: _plural_ κλᾷδες κλᾶδες codex)· ζυγά, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}:—old _Attic dialect_ **κλῄς**, κλῇδος, _accusative_ κλῇδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 212 (_anapaest meter_), 661 (Lyric poetry): κλείς and κλῄς}} in the same _Attic dialect_ Inscription, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1414.44}} and 47. (κλᾱϝῑς, cf. Latin _clavis, claudo._)
**1** **bar, bolt**, θύρας σταθμοῖσιν ἐπῆρσε (i.e. Hera, from within) κληῗδι κρυπτῇ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.168, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.241; κληῗδος ἱμάς [prev. work] 4.802, compare 838; ἐπὶ δὲ κληῗδ᾽ ἐτάνυσσεν ἱμάντι 1.442}}; ={ἐπιβλής}, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.455.}}
**2** **catch** or **hook**, passed through the door from the outside to catch the strap (ἱμάς) attached to the bar (ὀχεύς), ἐν δὲ κληῗδ᾽ ἧκε, θυρέων δ᾽ ἀνέκοπτεν ὀχῆας ἄντα τιτυσκομένη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.47, compare 50; οἴξασα κληῗδι θύρας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.89; δοιοὶ δ᾽ ἔντοσθεν ὀχῆες εἶχον ἐπημοιβοί, μία δὲ κληῒς ἐπαρήρει 12.456, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 1.14.}}
**3** later, **key**, τὴν κλεῖν ἐφέλκεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.13}}; κλεῖν παρακλείδιον a false **key**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 77: _plural_, κλῇδας οἶδα δώματος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 827, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 448; Λακωνικὴ κ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 343; κυριεύσοντα τῶν κ. “OGI” 229.56 (Smyrna, 3rd c.BC)}}; of a sacred **key** carried in processions, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“SIG” 900.14 (Panamara, 4th c.AD), 996.24 (Smyrna, perhaps 1st c.AD).}}
**4** _metaphorically_, Ἁσυχία βουλᾶν τε καὶ πολέμων ἔχοισα κλαῗδας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.4, compare 9.39}}; ἔστι κἀμοὶ κλῂς ἐπὶ γλώσσῃ, of silence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 316, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1052 (Lyric poetry); καθαρὰν ἀνοῖξαι κλῇδα φρενῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 661 (Lyric poetry)}}; κλῇδας γάμου φυλάττει, of Hera, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 976 (Lyric poetry)}}; of the **key** to a problem, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 179.4.}}
**II** **hook** or **tongue** of a clasp, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.294.}}
**II.2** **stop-cock**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “(Spiritalia) Pneumatica” 1.25.}}
**III** **collar-bone**, probably so called from its hook shape (see.[{{Refs 8th c.BC+|above 1.2), 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (only in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”), ὅθι κληῒς ἀποέργει αὐχένα τε στῆθός τε 8.325; κληῗδα παρ᾽ ὦμον πλῆξ᾽, ἀπὸ δ᾽ αὐχένος ὦμον ἐέργαθεν ἠδ᾽ ἀπὸ νώτου 5.146; ᾗ κληῗδες ἀπ᾽ ὤμων αὐχέν᾽ ἔχουσι 22.324, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 7, “Art.” 13; πᾳῖσον ἐμᾶς ὑπὸ κλῇδος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1035; τὴν κλεῖν συνετρίβην 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.61; τὴν κλεῖν κατεαγώς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.67: _plural_, 4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus Apoll.6, etc.; τὰ πλάγια καὶ τὰς κλεῖδας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 513b35; αἱ κλεῖδες (variant{κλεῖς}) καὶ αἱ πλευραί, of the crocodile, [prev. work] 516a28}}]; κλεῖδες ὀπταί roast **shoulder-bones** of the tunny (with play on {{Refs|1.3}}**visible** keys, opposed to κρυπταὶ κλεῖδες of the Laconians), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 7.2, compare 3rd c.BC: Diphilus Siphnius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.357a.}}
**IV** **rowing bench** in a ship, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”}} always in _plural_; ἐπὶ κληῗσι καθίζειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.419, etc.; κληΐδεσσιν ἐφήμενοι 12.215; once in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”, πεντήκοντ᾽ ἔσαν ἄνδρες ἐπὶ κληῗσιν 16.170; δησάμενοι.. ἐπὶ κληῗσιν ἐρετμά 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.37.}}
**V** of promontories, straits, etc., Κληῗδες or Κληΐδες τῆς Κύπρου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.108, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.6.3}}; πόντου κλῇδ᾽, of the Bosporus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 212 (Lyric poetry).}}
**VI** in _plural_, **sacred chaplets**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 256 (_anapaest meter_) (Ephes., _accusative_ to 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus).}}
**VII** in versification, **clausula, cadence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1127.}}

2808:
** κλείω**
**A**, _future_ κλείσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.20 (ἀπο-), 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 22.7}}; rare _future_ κατα-κλιῶ, see at {κατακλείω}: _aorist_ ἔκλεισα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.36, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 348b}}: _perfect_ κέκλεικα {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 18.4, LXX.1Ki.23.20, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 30}}: _pluperfect_ ἐκεκλείκειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Ἀννιβαϊκή” 47}}:*mdash;_middle_, _aorist 1_ ἐκλεισάμην (κατ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.5, (ἐγ-) [prev. author] “HG” 6.5.9:}} —_passive_, _future_ κλεισθήσομαι {{Refs|(συγ-) [prev. work] 5.2.19}}: _aorist_ ἐκλείσθην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.110}}, etc.: _perfect_ κέκλειμαι (later κέκλεισμαι _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 198) (see. below)}}:—_Ionic dialect_ **κληΐω** (ἀπο-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.7}}: _aorist_ ἐκλήῑσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.166, (ἐξ-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.144}}, _Epic dialect_ κλήῑσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.30}}; _infinitive_ κληῗσαι {{Refs|21.382:}} — _middle_, _future_ κληΐσσομαι conjecture in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 2.310}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἀπ-εκληΐσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.165, 3.55, 58}}: _perfect_ κεκλήϊμαι {{Refs|2.121.β, compare 3.117, 7.129 (with variants)}}: _pluperfect_ ἀπ-εκεκλέατο {{Refs|9.50}} codices:—old _Attic dialect_ **κλῄω** (also Trag.,{{Refs|compare “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 1.226)}}, _future_ κλῄσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.8}}: _aorist_ ἔκλῃσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1447 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.4, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 560c:}}_perfect_ κέκλῃκα (ἀπο-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1262}}:—_middle_, _future_ κεκλῄσομαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lys.” 1071}}: _aorist_ περι-κλῄσασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.52}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐκλῄσθην (κατ-, ξυν-) {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.117, 4.67}}, etc.: _perfect_ κέκλῃμαι (see. below):— _Doric dialect_ _future_ κλᾳξῶ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.32:}}_aorist_ ἀπό-κλᾳξον, -κλᾴξας, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] 15.43, 77, ἔκλᾳξε 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 7.2, compare κλάκαι (to be read κλᾷσαι) · κλεῖσαι, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}:—_middle_, _imperfect_ κατ-εκλᾴζετο {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 18.5}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ κατ-εκλᾴσθην {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.84}}, but _participle_ **συγκατα-κλαιχθείς**{{Refs|“Chron.Lind.D.” 62}}: _perfect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ κατα-κέκλᾱνται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 141.}} —Cf. κλῄζω (B). (κλείς):—**shut, close, bar**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (only in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”), κλήϊσεν δὲ θύρας}} **barred** the doors, {{Refs|21.387}}; ἐκλήϊσεν ὀχῆας **shot** the bars, so as to close the dooe door,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|24.166; κλῄειν πύλας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 997, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “R.” [same place], etc.; κ. πηκτὰ δωμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 479; κλεῖδες.., αἷς τὰς θύρας κλείουσιν 4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 7; Ἐτεοκλέους.. κλῄσας στόμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 865}}; κανθώς Cerc.[same place]; λάρυγγα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.65}}:—_passive_, βλέφαρα κέκλῃται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 711; ψυχῆς ἀνοῖξαι τὴν κεκλῃμένην πύλην [prev. author] “fragment” 393; κεκλειμένης σου τῆς παρρησίας οὐ κιγκλίσιν.., ἀλλὰ.. ὀφλήμασι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.28.}}
**A.2** **shut up, close, block up**, Βόσπορον κλῇσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 723 (troch.); κλῄσειν ταῖς ναυσὶν τοὺς ἔσπλους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.8}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121. ; τὰ ἐμπόρια κεκλῇσθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.14; κεκλειμένων τῶν ἐμπορίων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.16.}}
**A.II** **shut in, enclose**, πόλιν.. πύργων μηχανῇ κεκλῃμένην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 956}}; compare κλῄζω (B).
**A.III** **confine**, πλάστιγξ αὐχένα πώλων ἔκλῃε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 304}}:— _passive_, **to be confined**, χέρας βρόχοισι κεκλῃμένα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Andr.” 502 (Lyric poetry): _metaphorically_, ὅρκοις κεκλῄμεθα [prev. author] “Hel.” 977.}}
**A.III.2** **deliver bound**, τινὰ εἰς τὰς τοῦ βασιλέως χεῖρας {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.23.20.}}

2809:
** κλέμμα**, ατος, τό,
(κλέπτω) **thing stolen**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 618, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 952a19}}; **money equivalent of thing stolen**, τὸ κ. ἐκτείσας διπλοῦν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 857b, compare “Foed.Delph.Pell.” 2 A 14, “IG” 5(1).1390.77 (Andania, 1st c.BC).}}
**2** **theft**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 67 (_plural_, Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1203, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.53.}}
**II** **stratagem** in war, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.9}}; **fraud**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.31, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.100}}; κ. ἐρωτικόν **clandestine** amour, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 1.2, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 5.17 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus).}}

2811:
** κλέος**, τό,
_Doric dialect_ **κλέϝος** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“GDI” 1537 (Crissa, = Röhl “Imag.” 3pp.87/8 No.1), only _nominative_ and _accusative_ _sing._ and _plural_: _Epic dialect_ _plural_ κλέᾰ (before a vowel) 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below 11.1), κλεῖα (nisi to be read κλέεα) 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 100: (κλέω A)}}:— **rumour, report**, τί δὴ κ. ἔστ᾽ ἀνὰ ἄστ; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.461; κ. εὐρὺ φόνου 23.137; ὄσσαν.., ἥ τε μάλιστα φέρει κ. ἀνθρώποισι 1.283}}; σὸν κ. **news** of thee, {{Refs|13.415: with _genitive_, μετὰ κ. ἵκετ᾽ Ἀχαιῶν}}**the report** of their coming, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.227, compare 13.364}}; κείνου κατὰ κ. at **the news** of his coming, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.125; τῶν ἐμῶν κακῶν κ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 251}}; **rumour**, opposed to certainty, κ. οἶον ἀκούομεν οὐδέ τι ἴδμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.486; γυναικογήρυτον κ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 487 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II** **goodreport, fame**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, κ. ἐσθλόν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.3; ἀνδρὸς τοῦ κ. εὐρὺ καθ᾽ Ἑλλάδα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.344: _absolutely_, τῷ μὲν κ., ἄμμι δὲ πένθος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.197; τὸ δ᾽ ἐμὸν κ. οὔ ποτ᾽ ὀλεῖται 7.91, compare 2.325}}; κ. εἶναί τινι to be **a glory** to him,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|22.514; κ. οὐρανὸν ἵκει 8.192, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.20; κ. οὐρανὸν εὐρὺν ἵκανε 8.74; κ. ἄφθιτον 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20a. 4, 6th c.BC: Ibycus Lyricus “POxy.” 1790.47, “GDI”}}[same place]; κ. ἀρέσθαι, εὑρέσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.101, “P.” 3.111}}; γίνεσθε κατὰ κ. ὧδε μαχηταί in **renown**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“BCH” 24.71 (Acraeph., 3rd c.BC)}}; λαβεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1347}}; κ. αἰχμᾶς **glory in** or **for**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.66; τῆς μελλοῦς κ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1356}}; κ. σου μαντικόν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1098; μικροῦ δ᾽ ἀγῶνος οὐ μέγ᾽ ἔρχεται κ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 938: less frequently in Prose, κ. ἀέναον 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 29; μένοντι δὲ.. κ. μέγα ἐλείπετο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.220}}; κ. καταθέσθαι to lay up store of **glory**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.78; τιμὴν καὶ κ. ἔσχεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1035; πόρρω κ. ἥκει [prev. author] “Ach.” 646; κ. οὐρανόμηκες [prev. author] “Nu.” 459; κ. ἔχειν τὰ περὶ τὰς ναῦς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.25; παρ᾽ ἀνθρώποις ἀείμνηστον κ. ἔχει τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 1.6; κ. ἀθάνατον καταθέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 208c; κ. τε καὶ ἔπαινος πρὸς ἀνθρώπων [prev. author] “Lg.” 663a; περὶ χώρας ἀκούειν κ. μέγα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.5; κ. ἕξειν ἔν τινι 2nd c.BC: Athenaeus Mechanicus 15.4}}; ποῖον κ., εἰ..; {{NT|NT.1Pet.2.20: _plural_, ἄειδε δ᾽ ἄρα κλέα ἀνδρῶν}} **the lays** of their achievements, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.189, compare 524, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.73; κλέα φωτῶν μνήσομαι 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1.}}
**II.2** rarely in bad sense, δύσφαμον κ. ill **repute**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.36; αἰσχρὸν κ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 135, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 796: both senses in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.45 ἧς ἂν ἐπ᾽ ἐλάχιστον ἀρετῆς πέρι ἢ ψόγου.. κ. ᾖ}} of whom there is least **talk** either for praise or blame. (Cf. Sanskrit _śrávas_ 'fame', Slavonic _slovo_ 'word', 'glory'; _cognate_ with κλέω (A), κλύω.)

2812:
** κλέπτ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
**thief**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.11; τὸν πυρὸς κ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 946; κλέπτα δύο 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 928}}; opposed to ἅρπαξ (a robber), {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Myrtilus Comicus 4; λῃστὰς ἢ κλέπτας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 351c, cf. NT.John.10.8}}; ὁ τοῦ κ. λόγος, a logical fallacy, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 180b18.}}
**2** generally, **cheat, knave**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1135; κακῶν ἀλλοτρίων κ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.59.}}

2813:
** κλέπτω**,
_Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ κλέπτεσκον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.174}}: _future_ κλέψω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 667, etc., κλέψομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.13}}: _aorist_ ἔκλεψα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.268, etc.}}: _perfect_ κέκλοφα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 369, 372, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 941d}}; later _participle_ κεκλεβώς {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).1390.75 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}:—_passive_, _aorist 1_ ἐκλέφθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.84, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1580:}}_aorist 2_ ἐκλάπην [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 413b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 4.17}}; later _participle_ κλεπείς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 454.19 (2nd c.AD)}}: _perfect_ κέκλεμμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 681, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 57}}. (Cf. Latin _clèpere_, Gothic _hlifan_ (κλέπτειν), **hliftus** (κλέπτης)):—**steal**, with _accusative_ or _absolutely_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.24, 71, 109}}; τῆς γενεῆς ἔκλεψε from that breed Anchises **stole**, i.e. foals of that breed,{{Refs 6th c.BC+|5.268; κλέπτουσιν ἐφ᾽ ἁρπαγῇ ἄλλοθεν ἄλλος 6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 4.13; κ. μοιχεύειν τε 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 11.3; ἢν μηδὲν μήτε κλέπτῃ μήτε ἀδικῇ 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 253; κ. τι παρ᾽ ἀλλήλων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.186; κ. ἐξ ἱερῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 857b}}; **carry off**, κλέψεν Μήδειαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.250}}; πυρὸς σέλας κ., of Prometheus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 8; κλέψαι τε χἀρπάσαι βίᾳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 644}}; κ. τοὺς μηνύοντας **spirit away** the deponents, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.38}}; ἐξ ἐπάλξεων πλεκταῖσιν ἐς γῆν σῶμα κ. **let** it **down secretly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 958, compare 1010}}; κ. μορφάς, of painters, **steal** forms (by transferring them to canvas), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “epigram” 41.}}
**2** in _participle_ _active_, **thievish**, κλέπτον βλέπει he has a **thief s** look, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 900}}; κλέπτον τὸ χρῆμα τἀνδρός he's an arrant **thief**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 933.}}
**II** c._accusative_ _person_, **cozen, cheat**, πάρφασις, ἥ τ᾽ ἔκλεψε νόον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.217}}; οὐκ ἔστι Διὸς κλέψαι νόον Hes: {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Th.” 613; μὴ κλέπτε νόῳ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.132}}; κλέπτει νιν οὐ θεός, οὐ βροτός, ἔργοις οὔτε βουλαῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.29; σοφία κλέπτει παράγοισα μύθοις [prev. author] “N.” 7.23; οὔτοι φρέν᾽ ἂν κλέψειεν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 854, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 243, etc.; τὴν γνώμην 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.27; κ. τὴν ἀκρόασιν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.99}}:—_passive_, κλέπτεται ὁ ἀκροατής {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1408b5}}; προβαίνειν κλεπτόμενος to go on **blindfold**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.49}}; κλέπτεταί οἱ ἡ αὐγή his vision **becomes deceptive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.12; κλαπέντες ἢ βιασθέντες τοῦτο πάσχουσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “R.” [same place]: _impersonal_, κλέπτεται}} **the deception is passed off**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1404b24.}}
**III** **conceal, keep secret**, θεοῖο γόνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.36; θυμῷ δεῖμα [prev. author] “P.” 4.96}}; **disguise**, διαβολαῖς νέαις κλέψας τὰ πρόσθε σφάλματ᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 416; τοῖς ὀνόμασι κ. τὰ πράγματα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.142; τοὺς ἑαυτῶν κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitum magistro” 5.2; κ. ἑαυτὸν ὀφθαλμῶν τε καὶ ὤτων 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.7.2; κ. τοῦ διανοήματος τὴν ἄδειαν 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 239:}} —_passive_, κλέπτεται τὸ μετρικόν{{Refs 4th c.AD+|[prev. work] 182, compare 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “in Aristotelis Physica paraphrasis - pars” 276.26, 7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 6.103.}}
**IV** **do secretly** or **treacherously.** δόλοισι κ. σφαγάς **execute** slaughter **by secret** frauds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 37; πόλλ᾽ ἂν.. λάθρᾳ σὺ κλέψειας κακά [prev. author] “Aj.” 1137}}; κ. μύθους **whisper malicious** rumours, {{Refs|[prev. work] 188 (Lyric poetry)}}; κλέπτων ἢ βιαζόμενος **by fraud** or open force, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 933e; ταῦτα κλέπτοντες ταῖς πράξεσιν, i.e. λάθρᾳ πράττοντες, [prev. work] 910b}}; κλεπτομένη λαλιά **secret, clandestine**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 15, etc.}}
**IV.2** **seize** or **occupy secretly**, τὰ ὄρη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.9, compare 4.6.11, 15; τὴν ἀρχήν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.10.}}
**IV.3** **effect** or **bring about clandestinely**, γάμον κ. δώροις {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.151}}:—_passive_, **to be 'smuggled in**', {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1440b21.}}
**IV.4** **get rid of imperceptibly**, τὸ δοκεῖν.. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 8.7; τῇ ποικιλίᾳ τὸν κόρον [prev. author] “Comp.” 19}}:—_passive_, τοῦ πόσου κλεπτομένου {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.7.5.}}

2814:
** κλῆμα**, ατος, τό,
_Aeolic dialect_ **κλᾶμμα** (which see), **twig** or **branch**, especially **vinetwig**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1031, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.50, 6.3.8, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.5.5, “CP” 3.14.6, +others; ἀμπέλου κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 353a}}: generally, **cutting, slip**, ὁ βλαστὸς τοῦ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 19.8, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 550b8: _metaphorically_, ἀνατετμήκασί τινες τὰ κ. τὰ τοῦ δήμου D.cited in Aeschin. 3.166}}; of the navel string, πεῖσμα καὶ κ. τῷ γεννωμένῳ καρπῷ {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 148.}}
**2** **vine-switch, cane**, carried by Roman centurions, Latin _vitis_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 26, etc.}}
**II** ={πιτυοῦσσα}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.165}}; ={πολύγονον}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 27.113.}}
**III** ={ὑπόδημα}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; compare κλείματα.

2816:
** κληρονομ-έω**,
**inherit**, with _genitive_ _of things_, ὥσπερ τῆς οὐσίας, οὕτω καὶ τῆς φιλίας τῆς πατρικῆς κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.2, compare 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 4.7, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 127; ὅς γ᾽ ἐκεκληρονομήκεις τῶν.. χρημάτων πλεῖν ἢ πέντε ταλάντων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.312; μὴ πλειόνων ἢ μιᾶς [κληρονομίας] τὸν αὐτὸν κ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309a25: with _accusative_ _of things_, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 88, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 11.3, “BGU” 19 ii 1 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.: _absolutely_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 24.}}
**2** **acquire, obtain**, τὴν ἐπ᾽ ἀσεβείᾳ δόξαν {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.22.3; φήμην [prev. author] 18.55.8; θρόνον βασιλείας LXX.1Mac.2.57}}; τὴν γῆν **receive possession of** the promised land, Palestine,{{LXX|LXX.Lev.20.24, LXX.Deu.4.1}} (also, **obtain** all that God has promised,{{LXX+NT|LXX.Psa.36(37).11, cf. NT.Matt.5.5)}}; **obtain** salvation, ζωὴν αἰώνιον {{NT|NT.Matt.19.29.}}
**II** **to be an inheritor** or **heir**, τινος **of** a person, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 20}}: more frequently τινα, {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 36J., 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 2, “PGnom.” 5, +others (2nd c.AD), “Anthologia Graeca” 11.202, etc.; κ. τινὰ τῆς οὐσίας 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 45.47: _metaphorically_, σῆτες καὶ σκώληκες -ομήσουσιν αὐτόν LXX.Sir.19.3}}:—_passive_, **to be succeeded in the inheritance**, of parents, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.172, 291, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 22; ὑφ᾽ ὧν τὴν ῥητορικὴν ἐκληρονομήθη 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.26.6.}}
**III** **leave an heir behind** one, υἱοὺς υἱῶν {{LXX|LXX.Pro.13.22.}}

2817:
** κληρονομ-ία**, ἡ,
**inheritance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.43, etc.}}; ἡ κ. κατὰ τὴν ἀγχιστείαν **inheritance** as heir-at-law, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.3; κ. μὴκατὰδόσιν, ἀλλὰκατὰ γένος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309a23: _metaphorically_, εἰλήφασι τὴν τοῦ ὀνόματος κ. αἱ σωματικαὶ ἡδοναί}} have taken **possession** of.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1153b33.}}
**2** **property, possession**, ἀνέζευξεν ἕκαστος εἰς τὴν κ. αὐτοῦ {{LXX|LXX.Jdt.16.21(25), cf. LXX.1Mac.2.56, 6.24.}}

2819:
** κλῆρος**
**A**, _Doric dialect_ **κλᾶρος** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus (see. below), “Leg.Gort.” 5.27, etc.}}, ου, ὁ:— **lot**, κλῆρον ἐσημήναντο ἕκαστος, ἐν δ᾽ ἔβαλον κυνέῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.175; κλήρους ἐν κυνέῃ χαλκήρεϊ πάλλον 3.316, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.206; ἐκ κλῆρος ὄρουσεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.325; ἐκ δ᾽ ἔθορε κλῆρος κυνέης 7.182; ἐν δὲ κλήρους ἐβάλοντο 23.352; ἐπὶ κλήρους ἐβάλοντο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.209, compare “SIG” 1023.94 (from Cos)}}; κλήρῳ πεπαλάσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.331; κλήρῳ λάχον ἐνθάδ᾽ ἕπεσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.400, compare 23.862, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 187, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.83, etc.}}; πάντας ἀνέφεδρος ἐπαγκρατίασε τοὺς κ., i.e. he never drew a bye, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“SIG” 1073.29 (2nd c.AD)}}; κλήρου κατὰ μοῖραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 545 (Lyric poetry); διὰ τὴν τοῦ κ. τύχην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 619d, etc.}}; κλάροισι θεοπροπέων divining **by lots**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.190: hence, of oracles, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1057, “Ph.” 838; Ἑρμῆς γὰρ ὢν κλήρῳ ποιήσεις οἶδ᾽ ὅτι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 365; κ. Ἑρμοῦ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 39.}}
**A.2** **casting of lots, drawing of lots**, κ. τίθεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “IA” 1198, compare “Tr.” 186 (Lyric poetry); δοκεῖ δίκαιον εἶναι πᾶσι τῶν ἀρχῶν μετεῖναι ἐν τῷ κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 1.2, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1300a19, “IG” 5(1).1390.116 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}; = Latin _sortitio provinciarum_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 10.}}
**A.3** λαβὼν πίστιν.. κλήρου uncertain meaning in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 494.19 (1st-2nd c.AD).}}
**A.II** **that which is assigned by lot, allotment** of land, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.109, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.50, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 740b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1265b15, +others; λαβεῖν τᾶς χώρας ἐξαίρετον τὸν πρῶτον κλᾶρον “SIG” 141.6 (Corc.Nigr., 4th c.BC)}}; κ. ἱππικός {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 229.102 (Smyrna, 3rd c.BC)}}; περὶ τοῦ λάχους τριάκοντα καὶ ἑπτὰ κλάρων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 289.88 (Priene, 2nd c.BC), compare 313.4, +others}}
**A.II.2** generally, **piece of land, farm, estate**, οἶκος καὶ κ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.498; οἶκόν τε κ. τε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.64, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 37, 341, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.62; κατέφαγε τὸν κ. 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 35.4}}; οἱ κ. τῶν Συρίων their **lands**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.76, compare 9.94, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Delum” 281, etc.}}; Κύπρου Πάφου τ᾽ ἔχουσα.. κλῆρον, of Aphrodite, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 463; κατὰ κ. Ἰαόνιον [prev. author] “Pers.” 899 (Lyric poetry); κλῆροι χθονός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 876; τῶν λαβόντων ἐν Ὀρχομενῷ κλᾶρον ἢ οἰκίαν “IG” 5(2).344.12 (3rd c.BC),compare “SIG” 169.61 (Iasus, 4th c.BC)}}; Πισαίοις ἐνὶ κλήροισι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 74.5}}. b. _plural_, **title-deeds,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PGrenf. 1.14.11 (2nd c.BC).}}
**A.II.3** **legacy, inheritance, heritable estate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 923d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 9.2, “SIG” 1186 (4th c.BC), “IG” 22.1368.127, 154}}. b. _collective_, **body of inheritors,** {{Refs|Leges Gortynensium [prev. cited]}}
**A.II.4** Astrology texts, certain **degrees** in the zodiac connected with planets and important in a nativity, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 1.169, 170, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 111, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 59.21, +others, 4th c.AD: Paulus Alexandrinus Astrologus “K.” 2 (cf. Scholia); κ. τύχης 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 129.}}
**A.II.5** generally, **province, sphere**, ἕνα θεὸν πολλῶν ἅμα προεστάναι κλήρων {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 369.}}
**A.III** of the Levites, Κύριος αὐτὸς κλῆρος αὐτοῦ {{LXX|LXX.Deu.18.2}}: hence, of the Christian **clergy**, ἐν κλήρῳ καταλεγόμενος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 1.3.38.2, 6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 6.1.7, 2nd c.AD: Astrampsychus Onirocriticus “Oracula” 98.7.}}

2820:
** κληρ-όω**,
_Doric dialect_ **κλᾱρόω**, _infinitive_ κλαρώεν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Foed.Delph.Pell.” 1 A 6, κλαρώειν “SIG” 647.33 (Stiris, 2nd c.BC): (κλῆρος A)}}:—**appoint by lot**, ἐξ ἁπάντων τὰς ἀρχάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.22; ἀθλητάς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1393b5; διαιρετὰς τῶν κτημάτων “SIG” 364.9 (Ephesus, 3rd c.BC); τὰ δικαστήρια [prev. work]647.33; ἄλλον [ἱερόν] “IG” 5(1).1390.6(Andania, 1st c.BC)}}; also, of the lot, **fall on**, οὓς ἐκλήρωσεν πάλος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 416}}:—_middle_, **cast lots** for office, of candidates, ἂν ἔλθῃ κληρωσόμενος τῶν ἐννέα ἀρχόντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.4, compare 24.13; κ. ἱερωσύνης 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.62; κληρουμένων ἐπιμελῶς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 27.4; ὃς ἂν κληρούμενος λαγχάνῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 298e}}:—_passive_, **to be appointed by lot**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 43.2, Decrees [prev. work] 30.5, “SIG” 525.11 (Crete, 3rd c.BC), “IG” 5(1).1390.132 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}, etc.; [πρόεδροι] κεκληρωμένοι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.89; κεκληρῶσθαι ἄρχειν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Luct.” 2.}}
**2** **cast lots**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 759c, 856d}}; κληρώσω πάντας I **will make** all **draw lots**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 682; κ. τὰς φυλάς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.20.2}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 55, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 836, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.133}}; ὅτε ἐκληροῦσθε when **you were drawing lots**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 19.1.}}
**3** _middle_, **have allotted one, obtain by lot**, δεσπότας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 29; ἱερωσύνην 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.188; ἀμπέλων δεκανίαν “IGRom.” 4.1675 (from Lydia)}}: _metaphorically_, **obtain as one's sphere** or **province**, τὸ ταὐτὸν ὁ δημιουργὸς ἐκληρώσατο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 321; Astrology texts, ἥλιος κληρωσάμενος τὴν ὥραν 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 61.1}}; κεκληρῶσθαι **to be in possession of, to have**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 20, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 110; τὴν καρδίαν κεκλήρωται ἐπὶ τῇ φάρυγγι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 5.31.}}
**II** **allot, assign**, ὔμμε δ᾽ ἐκλάρωσε πότμος Ζηνί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.15; μοίρας, τὴν μὲν ἐπὶ μονῇ, τὴν δ᾽ ἐπὶ ἐξόδῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.94; ἓν ἑκάστῳ ἐκλήρωσαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.42}}:—_passive_, ἐκληρώθην δούλη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 100 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II.2** ὀμφὰν κ. **deliver** an oracle **by lot**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ion” 908 (Lyric poetry).}}

2821:
** κλῆσις**, εως, ἡ,
(καλέω) **calling, call**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 172a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.14, etc.}}
**2** **calling into court, summons, prosecution**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 875, 1189, etc.; τὰς κλήσεις καλεῖσθαι ὅσας ἔδει 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.38; ἀφιέναι τὰς κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.13.}}
**3** **invitation** to a feast, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 1.7; εἰς τὸ πρυτανεῖον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.32; κλήσεις δείπνων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 7, cf. Parmenisc. cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.156d.}}
**4** **invocation**, θεῶν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Men.Rh. p.333 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**5** **calling to aid**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.50.7.}}
**6** **calling** in a religious sense, {{NT|NT.1Cor.7.20.}}
**II** **name, appellation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 262d, 287e, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.42; τοὺς θεοὺς εἶναι κ. ἱεράς 4th-3rd c.BC: Cleanthes Stoicus 1.123; Φιλησίη τὴν κ.}}**by name,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.2067}}; **reputation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.46 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**III** Grammars, αἱ κ. τῶν ὀνομάτων the **nominatives**, opposed to αἱ πτώσεις (the oblique cases), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 48b41}}; ἔχειν θηλείας ἢ ἄρρενος κλῆσιν the **nominative form** of.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “SE” 173b40, compare 182a18.}}
**IV** = Latin _classis_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.18.}}

2822:
** κλητ-ός**, ή, όν,
**invited**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.162, etc.}}; **welcome**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.386.}}
**2** **called out, chosen**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.165.}}
**3** **invoked**, {{Refs|Anonymus cited in 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}
**4** **summoned to court,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PAmh. 2.79.5 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** _substantive_ κλητή (i.e. ἐκκλησία), ἡ, **convocation**, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.12.16, LXX.Lev.23.2 (_plural_).}}

2823:
Included with: ** κρίβᾰν-ος**, ὁ,
_Attic dialect_ for **κλίβανος** (which is called _Doric dialect_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“EM” 538.19, compare 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 143, and is the usually form in Papyrus, “PPetr.” 3p.328 (3rd c.BC), etc.)}}, **covered earthen vessel**, wider at bottom than at top, wherein bread was baked by putting hot embers round it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.92 (in form κλιβ-), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “fragment” 309, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ach.” 86, “V.” 1153, +others, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 176.5; οὕτως εἰμὶ ὡς εἰς κρίβανον “POxy.” 1842.7 (6th c.AD)}}; **potter's oven,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 271.9 (3rd c.BC, κλ-).}}
**2** **funnel-shaped vessel**, used for drawing water, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.2.13 (κλ-).}}
**II** **underground channel** or **vaulted passage**, in irrigation works, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Sammelb.” 7188.17 (2nd c.BC, κλ-).}}
**II.2** **hollow, cavern in a rock**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “NA” 2.22.}}

2824:
** κλίμα** [ῐ,
{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|compare 2nd c.BC: Scymnus Geographus 521]}}, ατος, τό, (κλίνω) **inclination, slope** of ground, ἑκάτερον τὸ κ. τῶν ὀρῶν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.16.3; ἡ πόλις τῷ ὅλῳ κ. τέτραπται πρὸς τὰς ἄρκτους [prev. author] 7.6.1, etc.}}; **scarp**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus “Abhandlungen der Göttinger Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften (Phil.-hist. Klasse)” 140.7.}}
**II** = {ἔγκλιμα}{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1.2, τοῦ κόσμου 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.2.22, compare 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 16.12, 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.2.}}
**II.2** terrestrial **latitude, latitudes, region**, τὸ μεσημβρινὸν κ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.9; τὸ ὑπάρκτιον κ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 11}}; τὰ πρὸς μεσημβρίαν κ. the southern **regions**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.44.6, compare 10.1.3, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.1.10, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.97 (1st c.AD: Alpheus Epigrammaticus), 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.523e, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 6.14, etc.; κ. οὐρανοῦ 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.11.4.}}
**II.3** **direction, cardinal point**, τὰ τέτταρα κ. (viz. N., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus, W.) 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.2.12, “Geoponica” 1.11.1, compare 6th-7th c.AD: Isidorus Hispalensis Grammaticus “Etym.” 13.1.3; τὸ νότιον κ. τοῦ κόσμου 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.365b; κατὰ τὸ βόρειον κ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 392a3.}}
**II.4** **seven latitudinal strips in the** οἰκουμένη **on which the longest day ranged by halfhour intervals from** {{Refs|13 to 16}}**hours**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Eratosthenes Epicus cited in 2nd c.BC: Scymnus Geographus 113, [prev. author] cited in 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.1.35, 2.5.34, 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 5.58, 16.17, 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus cited in 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Timaeum commentarii” 3.125 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator (compare [same work] cited in 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.10), [prev. author] cited in 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.2.1, 5th-6th c.AD: Marinus Biographus cited in 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Geographia” 1.15.8, 1.17.1, [prev. author] “Alm.” 2.12, +others, “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(4).37.}}
**II.5** **seven astrological zones** corresponding to Nos. 3-6 of κλίμα 11.4 {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC(?): Nechepso Astrologus “fragment” 5, +others, Vett. Val.22.33, +others, Firmic.2.11.2.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, **inclination, propensity**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.15.20.}}
**IV** **fall**, ἑπταετεῖ κλίματι **by death** at seven years of age, {{Refs|“IG” 14.2431.}}
**V** Grammars, **inflected form**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 173.25.}}
**VI** = {ὑπόδημα}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; compare κλείματα.

2825:
** κλῑν-η**, ἡ, κλίνω
) **that on which one lies, couch**, used at meals or for a bed, ἐν κλίνῃ κλῖναί τινας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.16, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1090}}; κλίνην στρῶσαι to make up a **couch**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.139, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.6, “IG” 22.1315; ἐπὶ κλίνης φερόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.61, compare “SIG” 1169.31 (Epid.)}}; ἐκ κλίνης ἀνίστασθαι, after illness, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.64; κ. μιλησιουργὴς ἀμφικέφαλος “IG” 12.330; κ. ἐπίχρυσοι καὶ ἐπάργυροι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.50, 9.80; κ. ἐλεφαντόποδες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 208.}}
**2** **bier**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.34, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 947b, “IG” 12(5).593.6 (Ceos, 5th c.BC).}}
**3** **grave-niche**, {{Refs|[prev. work]14.788 (from Naples), 871 (from Cumae).}}
**II** ἱερὰ κ., = Latin _lectisternium,_ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|POxy. 1144.6(1st-2nd c.AD),compare “PGnom.” 202 (2nd c.AD)}}; κ. τοῦ κυρίου Σαράπιδος, of a ceremonial **banquet,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 110.2 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** generally, **banquet**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 5.483.2 (_plural_, 3rd c.BC).}}

2826:
** κλῑν-ίδιον**, τό,
_diminutive_ of κλίνη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 916, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.68, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 17.6.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.751b}}; κ. κρεμαστόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 6.23.6, 1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in [same work] 6.25.4.}}
**2** = Latin _lectica_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 24.}}

2827:
** κλίνω** [ῑ],
_future_ κλῐνῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 557, (ἐγκατα-) 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 621}}: _aorist 1_ ἔκλῑνα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.37, etc.}}: _perfect_ κέκλῐκα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.13.2}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ ἐκλινάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.340, etc.:}} —_passive_, _future_ κλῐθήσομαι συγ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1090, (κατα-) 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 8 “fragment” 19}}: _future_ 2 κατα-κλῐνήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 98, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 222e, also κεκλίσομαι uncertain in 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 22.7}}: _aorist 1_ ἐκλίθην [ῐ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.470, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 101 (Lyric poetry), 1226, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 211 (_anapaest meter_), frequently in Prose; poetry also ἐκλίνθην, see below 11.1, 2, 3}}: _aorist 2_ ἐκλίνην [ῐ] only in compounds, κατακλῐνῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1208, 1211, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.15, etc.; ξυγκατακλῐνείς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 981:}}_perfect_ κέκλῐμαι (see. below); _infinitive_ κεκλίσθαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 325.3, but κεκλίνθαι variant [prev. work] 47.1. (κλῐ-ν-yω}}, for. root κλῐ: κλει-, compare κλειτύ; Sanskrit _śráyati_ 'cause to lean', 'support', Latin _clinare, clivus._):—**cause to lean, make to slope** or **slant**, ἐπὴν κλίνῃσι τάλαντα Ζεύς when he **inclines** or **turns** the scale, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.223}}; Τρῶας δ᾽ ἔκλιναν Δαναοί **made** them **give way**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5.37, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.59; ἐπεί ῥ᾽ ἔκλινε μάχην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.510; ἔκλινε γὰρ κέρας.. ἡμῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 704; also ἐκ πυθμένων ἔκλινε.. κλῇθρα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1262:}} — _middle_, Περσῶν κλινάμενοι [δύναμιν] {{Refs|“IG” 12.763.}}
**2** **make** one thing **slope** against another, i.e. **lean, rest** it, τι πρός τι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.171, compare 510; ἅρματα δ᾽ ἔκλιναν πρὸς ἐνώπια 8.435: with _dative_, ἔστησαν σάκε᾽ ὤμοισι κλίναντες}}, i.e. **raising** their shields **so that the upper rim rested** on their shoulders, {{Refs|11.593.}}
**3** **turn aside**, μηκέτι τοῦδε βήματος ἔξω πόδα κλίνῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 193 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὄσσε πάλιν κλίνασα **having turned** back her eyes, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.427}}; τὰς ἐκ τῶν ἀριστερῶν [φλέβας] ἐπὶ τὰ δεξιὰ κ. **turn** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 77e.}}
**4** **make** another **recline**, ἐν κλίνῃ κλῖναί τινας **make** them **lie down** at table, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.16; κλῖνόν μ᾽ ἐς εὐνήν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 227; κλίνατ᾽, οὐ σθένω ποσίν [prev. author] Alcaeus267 (Lyric poetry): _metaphorically_, ἡμέρα κλίνει τε κἀνάγει πάλιν ἅπαντα τἀνθρώπεια}} **puts to rest, lays low**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 131.}}
**5** in Magic, **make subservient**, ψυχήν {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.1718.}}
**6** Grammars, **inflect**, τὰ ῥήματα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 212.20}}:—_passive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pron.” 12.7.}}
**II** _passive_, **lean**, ἂψ δ᾽ ὁ πάϊς πρὸς κόλπον ἐϋζώνοιο τιθήνης ἐκλίνθη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.467}}; ὁ δ᾽ ἐκλίνθη, καὶ ἀλεύατο κῆρα μέλαιναν he **bent aside**, {{Refs|7.254}}; of a brasen foot-pan, ἂψ δ᾽ ἑτέρωσ᾽ ἐκλίθη **it was tipped over**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.470}}; of battle, **turn**, ἐκλίνθη δὲ μάχη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 711}}; of a body in equilibrium, οὐδαμόσε κλιθῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 109a, compare 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ τῶν ὀχουμένων” 1.8, +others}}
**II.2** **lean, stay oneself** upon or against a thing, with _dative_, ἀσπίσι κεκλιμένοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.135}}; κίονι, κλισμῷ κεκλιμένη, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.307, 17.97; ἠέρι δ᾽ ἔγχος ἐκέκλιτο καὶ ταχἔ ἵππω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.356 (assuming variant); ἐν δορὶ κεκλιμένος 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 2}} (also in _middle_, κλινάμενος σταθμῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.340); κεκλιμένοι καλῇσιν ἐπάλξεσιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.3; πρὸς τοῖχον ἐκλίνθησαν 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 34; ξύλα ἐς ἄλληλα κεκλιμένα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.73}}; ὅταν τύχωσι (i.e. αἱ ἄτομοἰ τῇ περιπλοκῇ κεκλιμέναι when they chance to be **propped** (i.e. **checked**) by the interlacing with others, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.8U.}}
**II.3** **lie down, fall**, ἐν νεκύεσσι κλινθήτην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.350, etc.}}; παραὶ λεχέεσσι κλιθῆναι **lie** beside her on the bed, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.213, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1226}}: in _perfect_, **to be laid, lie**, ἔντεα.. παρ᾽ αὐτοῖσι χθονὶ κέκλιτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.472}}; φύλλων κεκλιμένων of **fallen** leaves, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.194 (φύλλα κεκλ. in 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.9.2}}, **slanting** leaves); Ληθαίῳ κεκλιμένη πεδίῳ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1216}}; Ἀλφεοῦ πόρῳ κλιθείς **laid** by Alpheus' stream, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.92}}; ἐπὶ γόνυ κέκλιται **has fallen** on her knee, i.e.is humbled, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 931 (Lyric poetry); ὑπτία κλίνομαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1188; τὸ μὲν πρῶτον ἐρρήγνυτο τὸ τεῖχος, ἔπειτα δὲ καὶ ἐκλίνετο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.5; οὐ νούσῳ.. οὐδ᾽ ὑπὸ δυσμενέων δούρατι κεκλίμεθα “Anthologia Graeca” 7.493 (Antip. Thess.), compare 315 (4th-3rd c.BC: Zenodotus Grammaticus or 3rd c.BC: Rhianus Epicus), 488 (3rd c.BC: Mnasalcas Epigrammaticus), Epic. “Oxy.” 214r.3.}}
**II.4** **recline** at meals, κλιθέντες ἐδαίνυντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.211, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 543, “SIG” 1023.48 (Cos, 3rd-2nd c.BC)}}; κλίθητι καὶ πίωμεν conjecture in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Comica Adespota” 1203, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 691.}}
**II.5** of Places, **lie sloping towards** the sea, etc., **lie near**, ἁλὶ κεκλιμένη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.235; [νῆσοι] αἵ θ᾽ ἁλὶ κεκλίαται (_Epic dialect_ for κέκλινται) 4.608}}: hence, of persons, **lie on, live on** or **by**, [Ὀρέσβιος] λίμνῃ κεκλιμένος Κηφισίδι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.709; ῥηγμῖνι θαλάσσης κεκλίαται 16.68, compare 15.740; δισσαῖσιν ἀπείροις κλιθείς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 101 (Lyric poetry)}}; πλευρὰ πρὸς ἀνατολὰς κεκλιμένη, τὸ εἰς τὰς ἄρκτους κ., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.14.4, 1.42.5; ὄρος Κοῖον ὃ κέκλιται πρὸς Παρνασσόν “SIG” 826 E iii 37 (Delph., 2nd c.BC).}}
**II.6** _metaphorically_, τῷδε μέλει κλιθείς **having devoted himself to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.15}} (also in _active_, **incline** towards, τῶν πραγμάτων ἐπὶ Ῥωμαίους κεκλικότων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.13.2).}}
**II.7** **wander from the right course**, κεκλιμένη ναῦς {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 856.}}
**III** _middle_, **decline, wane**, καὶ κλίνεται (i.e. τὸ ἦμαρ) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 255.6.}}
**IV** _intransitive_ in _active_, κ. πρὸς τὸ ξανθὸν χρῶμα **incline** towards.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physiognomonica” 812b3}}; κλίνοντος ὑπὸ ζόφον ἠελίοιο **as** the sun **was declining**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.452}}; ἅμα τῷ κλῖναι τὸ τρίτον μέρος τῆς νυκτός as it **came to an end**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.93.7; ἡ ἡμέρα ἤρξατο κλίνειν NT.Luke.9.12; ἡ πόλις ἐπὶ τὸ χεῖρον ἔκλινεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.13; τὸ κλῖνον ἀναλήμψεσθαι “PFay.” 20.14 (3rd-4th c.AD).}}
**IV.2** of soldiers, κ. ἐπ᾽ ἀσπίδα, ἐπὶ δόρυ, **turn** to left, to right, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.115.9, etc.; κ. πρὸς φυγήν [prev. author] 1.27.8}}; also, **wheel**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 10.4.}}

2828:
** κλῐσία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, κλίνω) **place for lying down** or **reclining**: hence,
**I** **hut, shed, booth**, 1. for use in peace, **cot, cabin**, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”, 18.589, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.194, +others}}
**I.2** for use in war, **hut**, κ. εὔπηκτος, εὔτυκτος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.663, 10.566; κ. ὑψηλή 24.448}}: frequently in _plural_, **camp**, {{Refs|1.487, +others}}; πῦρ ἐν κλισίῃσι βαλόντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.501:—not common after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (σκηνή being used), 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.135, etc.}}: used by Trag. in Lyric poetry and _anapaest meter_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 131, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 190, 1407, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 189: later with various meanings, Βάκχου κλισίαι}}, of wine-**shops,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.889 (from Sinuessa)}}; εὐσεβέων κλισίη, of the grave, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“epigram Gr.” 237.4 (Smyrna, 2nd-1st c.BC),compare “IG” 12(5).1104 (Syros, 2nd c.AD)}}; **chapel**, ἡ κ. ἡ ἱερά {{Refs|“BCH” 51.220 (from Thasos), compare “Arch.Papyrus” 1.219, “IG” 42(1).123.131 (Epid.)}}; compare κλεισία.
**II** **anything for lying** or **sitting upon, couch** or **easy chair**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.123; κ. δινωτὴν ἐλέφαντι καὶ ἀργύρῳ 19.55; ἐπ᾽ ἀλλοτρίαν κ. ἐρχόμενος “IG” 22.1368.74.}}
**II.2** **couch for reclining on at table**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.133 (_plural_); ᾧ ξυνὴν εἶχον ἐγὼ κ. 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 1.1.8}}; **place on such couch**, κ. ἄτιμος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 59, 2.148f; κ. ἄδοξος Hegesander Historicus 18.}}
**II.3** **nuptial bed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 994, “IT” 857.}}
**III** **company** of people **sitting at meals,** {{NT|NT.Luke.9.14}}; **banquet**, εὐωχίαι τε καὶ κ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 35.5}}; **room for company**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 12.}}
**IV** **way of lying, decubitus**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 7.25; τὸ σχῆμα τῆς κ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sertorius” 26.}}

2829:
** κλοπ-ή**, ἡ,
(κλέπτω) **theft**, ἁρπαγῆς τε καὶ κλοπῆς δίκη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 534: _plural_, [prev. work] 402 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1175; κλοπῆς δίκη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 322a; ἱερῶν κλοπῆς δυοῖν ταλάντοιν γεγραμμένος 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.6, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 444, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 5d (_plural_); κλοπῆς ὀφλεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.74; ἐπὶ κλοπῇ χρημάτων ἀποκτείνειν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.25; κ. τῶν θησαυρῶν “PAmh.” 2.79.63 (2nd c.AD)}}; σκεῦος.. ἐκφέρειν ἐκ τοῦ ἱεροῦ ἐπὶ κλοπήν {{Refs|“SIG” 997.5 (from Smyrna)}}; κλοπῆς ἐν ταῖς εὐθύναις ἑάλωκεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.112, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 54.2, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 32}}; opposed to ἁρπαγή, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 941b.}}
**2** of authors, **plagiarism**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus cited in 4th c.AD: Eusebius Caesariensis Scriptor Ecclesiasticus “Praeparatio Evangelica” 10.3.}}
**II** **secret act** or **transaction, fraud**, κλέπτουσα μύθοις κλοπάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 100; πράγματος μεγάλου κ. 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.57}}; κλοπῇ **by stealth** or **fraud**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1025, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1254}}; ποδοῖν κλοπὰν ἀρέσθαι, i.e. to steal away, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 246 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** in warfare, **surprise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.14.}}

2830:
** κλύδων** [ῠ], ωνος, ὁ,
**wave, billow**, and collectively, **surf, rough water**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.421; πόντιος κ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 431 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1687 (Lyric poetry)}}; κ. πελάγιος, θαλάσσιος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 701, “Med.” 29; Θρῄκιος κ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 197 (Lyric poetry); κ. ἄγριος 5th-6th c.BC: Timotheus Lyricus “Persae” 146: in Prose, probably in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.84 (9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus, κλυδωνίῳ codices),compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 25.2; πνεῦμα καὶ κ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 548b13; κ. καὶ χειμών [prev. author] “PA” 685a32: _plural_, 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 474, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.10.3.}}
**2** in Medicine texts, **splashing** in the stomach and chest, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.348, +others}}; of sound heard in pleurisy, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.285}}; ἢν κ. ὑγρῶν ἀναπνέῃ ἐς τὰς διαπνοάς **flood** of humours, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.5}}; ofinternal water in dropsy, {{Refs|[prev. author] “SD” 2.1.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, κ. κακῶν **sea** of troubles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 599; κ. ξυμφορᾶς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1527 (troch.)}}; κ. ἔφιππος **flood** of chariots, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “El.” 733; πολέμιος κ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 60; πολὺς κ. δορός [prev. author] “Supp.” 474; ἔριδος κ. [prev. author] “Hec.” 116 (_anapaest meter_); πόλις ἐν κλύδωνι τῶν ἄλλων πόλεων διαγομένη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 758a; κ. καὶ μανία 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.314; ἐν χειμῶνι πολλῷ καὶ κ. τῆς πόλεως 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 32, compare 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 12.14; κ. ἀλογίας 5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 26p.479M.}}

2833:
** κνήθ-ω**,
later form of κνάω, **scratch**, ὡς λέγεται, κνήθειν οἶδεν ὄνος τὸν ὄνον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.238.8 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus), cf. Moer. p.234 P.:}} —_middle_, κνήθεσθαι εἰς τὰς ἀκάνθας τὰ ἕλκη **to get** one's sores **scratched**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 609a32.}}
**2** _passive_, **itch**, Paul.{{NT|Aeg. 6.60; κνηθόμενοι τὴν ἀκοήν NT.2Tim.4.3}}; **to be provoked**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 957b15.}}

2836:
** κοιλί-α**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κοιλί-η**, ἡ, (κοῖλος) **cavity of the body**, i.e. **thorax with abdomen**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 46 (including ἡ ἄνω κ.}}, = **thorax**, ἡ κάτω, = **abdomen**, _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.896)}}; τὰ κατὰ κ. νουσήματα diseases of the **thoracic cavity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ παθῶν” 6.}}
**2** **belly, abdomen**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.87, “IG” 42(1).122.32 (Epid.), etc.: specified as ἡ κάτω κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 485, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἑλκῶν” 3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 73a, 85e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Somno et Vigilia” 456a3, “PA” 650a13, etc.}}; opposed to ἡ ἄνω κ., **stomach**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 85e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “PA”}}[same place]; κ. alone frequently = **stomach**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 489a2, etc.}}; of birds, {{Refs|[prev. author] “PA” 674b22}}; also, **paunch** or **rumen** of animals, {{NT|[prev. author] “HA” 507b5: hence, of gluttons, δουλεύειν τῇ ἑαυτῶν κ. NT.Rom.16.18, cf. NT.Phil.3.19.}}
**3** **intestines**, κ. κείνη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.40, compare 86, 92}}, etc.; of animals, κ. ὑεία pig's **tripe**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 356; κοιλίας ἥμισυ “SIG” 1025.51 (Cos, 4th-3rd c.BC)}}: _plural_, **tripe and puddings**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 160, “Pl.” 1169.}}
**3.b** phrases, κ. σκληρὰν ἔχειν to be costive, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 62.2; κατὰ κοιλίαν νοσεῖν “Comica Adespota” 730}}; τὴν κ. λύειν to relax the **bowels**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 863b29, 864b14}}; αἱ κ. λύονται, ἀναλύονται,{{Refs|[prev. work] 947b13, “GA” 728a15}}; εὔλυτοί [εἰσι] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pr.” 876b31; ἐὰν ἡ κ. στῇ [prev. author] “HA” 588a7; κ. καταρραγεῖσα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 126}}; [οἶνος] κοιλίας μαλακτικός, κοιλίας ἐφεκτικά, Mnesith. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.33b, 2.59c}}; κ. ἐκλύειν, ὑπάγειν, μαλάσσειν, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.72, 163, 171; κ. ῥέουσαι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.41.}}
**4** **excrement**, especiallyin _plural_, κ. συνεστηκυῖαι **excrements** of firm consistency, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 10; opposed to κ. ἐφυγραινόμεναι [prev. author] “Epid.” 1.10; κ. ὑγρή [prev. author] “Prorrh.” 1.38; στερεή, σκληρή, [prev. author] “Acut.(Sp.”) 56, “Epid.” 4.23; οὔρησις καὶ κ. ἀχρόως [prev. passage]}}
**II** any **cavity** in the body, **ventricle, chamber**, as in the **lungs, heart, liver, brain**, κ. αἱ τὸ πνεῦμα δεχόμεναι καὶ προπέμπουσαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Art.” 41; ἡ δὲ καρδία ἔχει μὲν τρεῖς κ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 496a4, compare 513a27.}}
**II.2** **socket** of a bone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 61.}}
**II.3** supposed **cavities inside the muscles**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Erasistratus Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 4.375, 707, 2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 8.6.30, 7.9.4}}; compare νηδύς.
**II.4** **womb**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.38, +others, NT.John.3.4.}}
**III** any **hollow** or **cavity**, in the earth, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 349b4, 350b23, +others}}; in the clouds,{{Refs|[prev. work] 369b2, +others}}
**IV** perhaps finger-**tip**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.8 (_plural_).}}

2837:
** κοιμ-άω**,
_future_ -ήσω, _Doric dialect_ -άσω [ᾱ]: _aorist_ ἐκοίμησα, _Epic dialect_ κοίμησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.372}}:—_middle_, _future_ -ήσομαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“OGI” 383.43 (Commagene, 1st c.BC), 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.64, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 4.4, etc.: _Epic dialect_}}_aorist_ κοιμήσατο, -αντο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.241, 1.476}}:—_passive_, _future_ -ηθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 574.6, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 40, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.37.3, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐκοιμήθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.411, +others, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 390, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 571e, etc.}}: _perfect_ κεκοίμημαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschrio Lyricus 8.2, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Gall.” 6}}:—**lull, put to sleep**, κοίμησον.. Ζηνὸς ὑπ᾽ ὀφρύσιν ὄσσε φαεινώ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.236; ἦ με.. κοιμήσατε νηλέϊ ὕπνῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.372; βλέφαρα μὴ κοιμῶν ὕπνῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 3}}; **put to bed**, τὸν δ᾽ αὐτοῦ κοίμησε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.397}}; of a hind, ἐν ξυλόχῳ.. νεβροὺς κοιμήσασα {{Refs|4.336.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **still, calm**, ἀνέμους, κύματα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.281, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.169; φλόγα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 597; κύματος μένος [prev. author] “Eu.” 832; εὔφημον.. κοίμησον στόμα [prev. author] “Ag.” 1247}}; also, **soothe, assuage**, κοίμησον δ᾽ ὀδύνας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.524}}; ᾧ (i.e. φύλλῳ) κοιμῶ τόδ᾽ ἕλκος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 650.}}
**II** _middle_ and _passive_, **fall asleep, go to bed**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.476, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9, etc.}}; of animals, **lie down**, κατὰ ἤθεα κοιμηθῆναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.411: with _accusative_ _cognate_, ποῖόν τινα ὕπνον ἐκοιμ; 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 6.7}}; βαθὺν κοιμηθῆναι (i.e. ὕπνον) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMar.” 2.3.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, ὅπως ἂν κοιμηθῇ [τὸ ἐπιθυμητικόν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus [prev. cited]}}
**II.3** of the sleep of death, κοιμήσατο χάλκεον ὕπνον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.241; ἱερὸν ὕπνον κ. Callias Epigrammaticus “Ep.” 11.2}}: _absolutely_, **fall asleep, die**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 509 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Aeschrio Lyricus [same place]; ἐκοιμήθη μετὰ τῶν πατέρων LXX.3Ki.2.10, +others, compare “PFay.” 22.28 (1st c.AD), NT.Matt.27.52, NT.John.11.11, etc.; in epitaphs, “IG” 14.1683, etc.; κ. τὸν αἰώνιον ὕπνον [prev. work]929.}}
**II.4** κοιμῶντο.. παρὰ μνηστῇς ἀλόχοισι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.246, compare 250}}: hence, of sexual intercourse, **lie with** another, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.295, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 8(7).23; οὔ τινι κοιμηθεῖσα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 213; παρά τινι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.68; σὺν δεσπόταισι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus [same place]; μετά τινος 4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 22.2; ἀπὸ γυναικὸς ἀνὴρ τὰν νύκτα κοιμαθές “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.157 (from Cyrene).}}
**II.5** **keep watch at night**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.4, 9, “POxy.” 933.25 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**II.6** of things, **remain during the night**, οὐ μὴ κοιμηθήσεται ὁ μισθὸς παρά σοι ἕως πρωί {{LXX|LXX.Lev.19.13}}; ἡ κιβωτὸς ἐκοιμήθη ἐκεῖ{{LXX|LXX.Josh.6.10.}}
**II.7** with _accusative_, **dream of**, μέταλλα χρύσεια {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Gall.” 6.}}

2838:
** κοίμ-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**lying down to sleep**, κοιμήσεις ἐπὶ θύραις {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 183a; ἡ κ. τοῦ ὕπνου NT.John.11.13.}}
**II** the **sleep of death**, {{LXX+3rd c.AD+|LXX.Sir.46.19, 48.13, “Tab.Defix.Aud.” 242.30 (Carthage, 3rd c.AD).}}

2839:
** κοινός**, ή, όν,
also ός, όν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 207 (Lyric poetry)}}:— **common** (opposed to ἴδιος), not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. ξυνός)}}; ἐκ κοινοῦ **shared in common**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 723; ἔσται γὰρ βίος ἐκ κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 610}}; of a **common** altar, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 140; τὸ τέμενος εἶναι κ. “SIG” 1044.29 (Halic., 4th-3rd c.BC)}}; κ. ἔρχεται κῦμ᾽ Ἀΐδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.30}}; τρεῖς.. κ. ὄμμ᾽ ἐκτημέναι, of the Gorgons, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 795}}; κ. ὠφέλημα θνητοῖσιν φανείς, of Prometheus,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 613; τὰς γυναῖκας εἶναι κοινάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 457d: proverbial, κοινὸν τύχη 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 389, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 356; κοινὰ τὰ τῶν φίλων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 735 (troch.), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 279c, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 9, etc.}}; κ. Ἑρμῆς 'share the luck', {{Refs|[prev. author] “Epit.” 67, 100}}; κ. ἀρωγά **common** aid (i.e. for all), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1145 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐν δὲ κοινὸς ἀρσένων ἴτω κλαγγά and let the shouts of males rise **jointly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 207 (Lyric poetry); κ. πόλεμον πολεμεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 2.8; τὸν ἀέρα τὸν κ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 531.8; κ. τὸν ᾅδην ἔσχον οἱ πάντες βροτοί [prev. author] 538.8; κ. ἀγαθὸν τοῦτ᾽ ἐστί, χρηστὸς εὐτυχῶν [prev. author] 791: with _dative_, κ. τινί}} **common to** or **with** another, ὑμῖν φῶς.. καὶ τοῖσδ᾽ ἅπασι κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 523; ὁ δαίμων κ. ἦν ἀμφοῖν ἅμα [prev. author] “Th.” 812; θάλατταν κ. ἐᾶν τοῖς ἡττημένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.19; οἰκία.. κοινοτάτη ἀεὶ τῷ δεομένῳ [prev. author] 1.147}}; [πολιτεία] τίς κοινοτάτ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1289b14, compare 1265b29; κοινόν τι χαρᾷ καὶ λύπῃ δάκρυα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.32; τὸν ἥλιον τὸν κ. ἡμῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 611: with _genitive_, πάντων αἰθὴρ κ. φάος εἱλίσσων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1092 (_anapaest meter_), compare “Pers.” 132 (Lyric poetry), “Eu.” 109, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.32}}; κ. τῶν Λακεδαιμονίων τε καὶ Ἀθηναίων **shared in by** both.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 241c, etc.: with Preps., τὸ ἐπὶ πᾶσι κ.}}, see below see; κ. κατ᾽ ἀμφοτέρων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 144.19; οὐ γίγνεταί μοί τι κ. πρός τινα “Anthologia Graeca” 11.141 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus), compare 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 5.7}}; μέρος κ. πρός τινα **shared** with.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“CPR” 22.11 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.; κ. μεταξύ τινων {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Stud.Pal.” 1.7 ii 11 (5th c.AD).}}
**II** in social and political relations, **public, general**, τὸ κ. ἀγαθόν the **common** weal, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.90; κ. λόγῳ [prev. author] 5.37, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.141}}; κ. στόλῳ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 170; ἀδικήματα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.45; ὁ τῆς πόλεως κ. δήμιος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 872b}}; κοινότατον **of public** or **general interest**,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 724b, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1354b29}}; of constitutions, **popular, free**, κοινοτέραν εἶναι τὴν ἐκείνου μοναρχίαν τῆς αὑτῶν δημοκρατίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.36.}}
**II.2** τὸ κ. the **state**, τὸ κ. Σπαρτιητέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.67: _absolutely_, of one's own state, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 208, etc.; τὸ κ. ὠφελεῖται 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.20; τὰς ὠφελείας ἅπασιν εἰς τὸ κ. ἀπεδίδου 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.36.}}
**II.2.b** especially of **leagues** or **federations**, τὸ κ. τῶν Ἰώνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.109; τῶν συμμάχων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.21; τῶν Βοιωτῶν “SIG” 457.10 (Thespiae, 3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 20.6.1 (_plural_)}}, etc.; ἄνευ τοῦ πάντων κοινοῦ (i.e. τῶν Θεσσαλῶν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.78}}; also, of **private associations,** {{Refs|Test.Epict. 1.22, “SIG” 1113 (from Loryma), +others}}; of **guilds** or **corporations**, τὸ κ. τῶν τεκτόνων {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 53.2 (4th c.AD)}}; of **boards** of magistrates, τὸ κ. τῶν ἀρχόντων {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. work] 54.12 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.2.c** **the government, public authorities**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.90, 2.12, etc.; τὰ κ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.156; ἀπαγγεῖλαι ἐπὶ τὰ κ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.37}}; ἀπὸ τοῦ κ. **by public authority**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.85, 8.135}}; σὺν τῷ κ. **by common consent**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.87.}}
**II.2.d** **the public treasury**, χρημάτων μεγάλων ἐν τῷ κ. γενομένων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.144; ἐν τῷ κ. καὶ ἐν τοῖς ίεροῖς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.6, compare 17; χρήματα δοῦναι ἐκ τοῦ κ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.87}}; ἔχειν ἐν κοινῷ (without the _Article_), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.80, cf. Scholia (at [prev. work])}}
**II.2.e** **common right** or **rights** of citizens, τὸ κ. τὸ τῶν πολιτῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1283b41.}}
**II.3** τὰ κ. **public affairs**: πρὸς τὰ κ. προσελθεῖν, προσιέναι, to enter **public life**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.257, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.165}}; but also, **the public money**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 569, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.23 (in full, τὰ κ. χρήματα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.34, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1271b11)}}; τὰ κ. τῆς πόλεως, opposed to τὰ ἁγνά, {{Refs|“BMus.Inscription” 4.481}}*.383; ἀπὸ κοινοῦ **at the public expense**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.27, 5.1.12; ἐκ κοινοῦ φαγεῖν 3rd c.BC: Euphro Comicus 8.4, compare 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 230}}; ἐκ κ. from **common funds**, at **joint expense,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PGrenf. 1.21.19 (2nd c.BC).}}
**III** **common, ordinary**, τὰ κ. εἰδέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 366b; διὰ τῶν κ. ποιεῖσθαι τὰς πίστεις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1355a27}}; κοινοτάτη τῶν αἰσθήσεων [ἡ ἁφή] {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1118b1}}; τὰ κ. **commonplaces**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 27, “Epit.” 309; so κ. τόπος 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “προγυμνάσματα” 6, 4th-5th c.AD: Aphthonius Rhetor “Προγυμνάσματα - Rhet.” 7}}; ἡ κ. ἔννοια or ἐπίνοια, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.62.2, 6.5.2}}; κ. νοῦς, φρένες, **common** sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.37 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 202 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; κ. καὶ διήκουσαι κακίαι **general** and all-pervading vices, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Sign.” 28; κ. καὶ δημώδη ὀνόματα 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 40.2; κ. καὶ ἐν μέσῳ κείμενα ὀνόματα 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Lysia” 3}}; ἡ κ. διάλεκτος **every-day** language (free from archaisms and far-fetched expressions), {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Isoc.” 2; πεφευγὼς τὸ κ. 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Acad. Ind.” p.53 M.}}
**III.2** Grammars, **ordinary, 'regular' Greek**, opposed to special dialects, διάλεκτοί εἰσι πέντε, Ἀτθὶς Δωρὶς Αἰολὶς Ἰὰς καὶ κ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Scholia 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus p.14 H., compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.16, 4th c.AD(?): Theodosius Alexandrinus Grammaticus “Canones - Grammatici Graeci, pars” p.37}} H., etc.; ἡ κ. alone, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Conjunctionibus;” 223.24}}; τὸ κ. ἔθος, ἡ κ. ἐκφορά, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Adv.” 155.10, “Pron.” 4.27}}; οἱ κ. the writers **who use this language**,{{Refs|Scholia 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus p.469 H., “Etymologicum Magnum” 405.23.}}
**III.2.b** **colloquial, vulgar Greek**, Moer.{{Refs|pp.201 (_comparative_, probably to be read instead of καιν-), 243 P., +others}}
**III.2.c** ἡ κ. διάλεκτος **demotic** Egyptian, Manethoap. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “contra Apionem” 1.14.}}
**III.3** **common, of inferior quality**, χρυσός {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 905.5 (2nd c.AD), 1273.6 (3rd c.AD).}}
**III.4** in magical formulae, of **words added at will** by the user, '**and so forth**', frequently in Papyrus, {{Refs|“PMag.Osl.” 1.255, “PMag.Par.” 1.273, +others; κοινὰ ὅσα θέλεις [prev. work]2.53; ὁ κ. λόγος “PMag.Lond.” 46.435}}; compare κοινολογία.
**IV** of Persons, **connected by common origin** or **kindred**, especiallyof brothers and sisters, κ. σπέρμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.92, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 261, “OC” 535 (Lyric poetry); κ. αἷμα [prev. author] “Ant.” 202, compare 1}}; κ. πατήρ, μήτηρ, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PAmh.” 2.152.9 (5th-6th c.AD), “PFlor.” 47.11 (3rd c.AD)}}; also κ. Χάριτες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.50.}}
**IV.2** **one who shares in** a thing, **partner**, ἐν θύμασιν κ. ποεῖσθαί τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 240; κ. ἐν κοινοῖσι λυπεῖσθαι [prev. author] “Aj.” 267, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 917}}; also κ. τῷ θεῷ **belonging in part** to the god (who claims tithe of his substance), {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.161 (from Cyrene).}}
**IV.3** **lending a ready ear to all, impartial**, μὴ οὐ κ. ἀποβῆτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.53}}; **neutral**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 68; κοινοὺς τῷ τε διώκοντι καὶ τῷ φεύγοντι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 15.1; μέτριος καὶ κ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 6.3}}; κοινοί, οἱ, **arbitrators**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“GDI” 1832.10 (Delph.); κ. μεσίτης “PStrassb.” 41.14 (3rd c.AD)}}; of a capital city, δεῖ.. κοινὴν εἶναι τῶν τόπων ἁπάντων **easily accessible** on all sides, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1327a6.}}
**IV.3.b** **courteous, affable**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 13.9; κ. ἅπασι γενέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.80; τῇ πρὸς πάντας φιλανθρωπίᾳ κ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Demochares Historicus 2 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; ἔχειν τὰς κ. φρένας 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.202 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**IV.3.c** in bad sense, κοινή, ἡ, **prostitute**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 119.30, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Hist.Phil.” 12 (_plural_).}}
**IV.3.d** of events, κοινότεραι τύχαι **more impartial**, i.e. **more equal**, chances, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.102}}; ἔστιν ἐν τῷ κ. πᾶσι with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.6.}}
**V** in Logic, **general, universal**, τὸ κ. λαμβάνειν περί τινων, τὸ ἐπὶ πᾶσι κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 185b, 185c; τὰ κ. λεγόμενα ἀξιώματα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Posteriora” 76b14; αἱ κ. ἀρχαί [prev. work] 88a36}}; κ. ἔννοιαι **axioms**, heading in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra}}; **general**, κ. ὅρος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 987b6}}; κοινὰ καὶ στοιχειώδη **general** principles, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.69 S}}.; κ. σημεῖον, opposed to ἴδιον, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sign.” 14}}; κ. κρίσις **objectively valid** judgement, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Po.” 5.22; ὄνομα κ. 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.2.10}}; **abstract**, ὁ κ. ἄνθρωπος καὶ λογισμῷ ληπτός {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 341.}}
**VI** Grammars,
**VI.1** κ. συλλαβή **common** syllable, capable of being long or short, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 633.17, 7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 1.4.}}
**VI.1.b** κ. ποιήματα, poems which are both κατὰ στίχον and συστηματικά, e.g. the Sapphic stanza, {{Refs|[prev. author] pp.58,59}} C.; also, poems **of ambiguous metrical form**, {{Refs|[prev. author] p.60 C.}}
**VI.2** see.above{{Refs|111.2.}}
**VI.3** of gender, κ. γένος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 634.19}}; of nouns, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 30.7, +others, “Etymologicum Magnum” 143.33, 305.19, etc.}}
**VI.4** ἀπὸ κοινοῦ λαμβάνειν, of two clauses taking a word **in common**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 122.14, +others; κοινὸν or ἐκ κοινοῦ παραλαμβάνεσθαι, [prev. work] 20, 28, +others}}
**VII** of forbidden meats, **common, profane**, φαγεῖν κ. καὶ ἀκάθαρτον {{NT|NT.Act.10.14, cf. NT.Rom.14.14; κ. χερσὶ ἐσθίειν NT.Mark.7.2.}}
**VIII** κοινόν, τό, name of an **eye-salve**, {{Refs|“CIL” 13.10021.3, +others}}
**B** _adverb_ κοινῶς **in common, jointly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1462; τὰ κοινὰ κ. δεῖ φέρειν συμπτώματα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 817: _comparative_, ἐν Κρήτῃ -οτέρως [ἔχει τὰ τῶν συσσιτίων] 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1272a16.}}
**B.2** **publicly**, κ. μᾶλλον ὠφέλησαν ἢ ἐκ τῶν ἰδίων ἔβλαψαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.42, etc.}}
**B.3** **sociably, like other citizens**, οὐδὲ κ. οὐδὲ πολιτικῶς ἐβίωσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.151; ἴσως καὶ κ. πρός τινα προσφέρεσθαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1430a1; κ. καὶ φιλικῶς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 33}}; μετρίως καὶ κ. ὰσπάζεσθαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Arat.” 43.}}
**B.4** **in general**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Diphilus Siphnius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.81a}}; ἡ κ. σύνεσις, τὸ κ. ἄνθρωπον”, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.34J., “Mort.” 38}}; opposed to ἰδίως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus “Herc.” 1014.41, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marcellus” 8, compare 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 15.1; κοινότερον εἰπεῖν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.256 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; -οτέρως {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “fragment” 93.}}
**B.5** **in the common dialect**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 82.27, +others}}: _comparative_ -ότερον {{Refs|[prev. author] “Synt.” 159.5.}}
**B.6** **in plain language**, opposed to σοφιστικῶς, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.659f}}; **in the ordinary** or **wide sense**, opposed to κυρίως, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Aristotelis Analyticorum Posteriorum paraphrasis - pars” 5.5}}: _comparative_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 2.10.}}
**B.II** _feminine_ _dative_ **κοινῇ**; _Doric dialect_ **κοινᾷ** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 56.11 (Argos, 5th c.BC)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **κυνῆ** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work]635.31 (Acraeph., 2nd c.BC)}}:—**in common, by common consent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.148, 3.79, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 606, “OC” 1339, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 731, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3, etc.; κ. πᾶσι καὶ χωρίς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1278b23, compare “Ath.” 40.3}}; κ. μετά τινος, κ. σύν τινι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 209c, “SIG” 346.27 (4th c.BC), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.14, etc.; ἰδίᾳ τε καὶ κ. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 291: also _neuter_ _plural_ κοινά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 546.}}
**B.II.2** **publicly**, καὶ κ. καὶ ἰδίᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.2.10, “Mem.” 2.1.12, etc.}}
**B.II.3** as _preposition_ with _dative_, **together with**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1228, “Hel.” 829, “fragment” 823.}}
**B.III** with Preps., εἰς κοινόν **in common, in public**, ὑμῖν τῇδέ τ᾽ ἐς κ. φράσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 844; πᾶσιν ἐς κ. λέγω [prev. author] “Eu.” 408, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 457 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 796e; εἰς κ. γνώμην ἀποφαίνεσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.156}}; εἰς τὸ κ. λέγειν, ἀγορεύειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 165a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.27}}; εἰς τὸ κ. **for public use**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 681c.}}
**B.III.2** ἀπὸ κοινοῦ, ἐκ κοινοῦ, see.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.1, 11.3, VI.4.}}
**B.III.3** ἀφεῖσαν ἐν κοινῷ ζητεῖν, Latin _rem in medio reliquerunt_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 987b14}}; but οἱ ἐν κ. γιγνόμενοι λόγοι, ={οἱ ἐξωτερικοὶ λόγοι}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “de An.” 407b29.}}
**B.III.4** κατὰ κοινόν, opposed to κατ᾽ ἰδίαν, **jointly, in common**, Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.94, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.3.5; probably to be read instead of κατὰ κοινοῦ [prev. author] 11.30.3.}}

2840:
** κοιν-όω**,
_future_ κοινώσω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 673}}: _aorist_ ἐκοίνωσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 889d}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐκοίνᾱσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.115}}:—_middle_, _future_ κοινώσομαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “N.” 3.12}} codices (to be read -άσομαι (_Doric dialect_)), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 499}}: _aorist_ ἐκοινωσάμην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1347, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 11.50, etc.}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ἐκοινώθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 38, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 59b:}}_perfect_ κεκοίνωμαι (in _middle_ sense) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 493}}:—**communicate, impart** information, κ. τινί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 717 (in 673 an _accusative_ must be supplied), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 685, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 197, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.4, etc.; μῦθον ἔς τινας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 44 (_anapaest meter_); κ. τινὶ περί τινος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 369}}; νυκτὶ κοινάσαντες ὁδόν **having imparted** their journey to night alone (i.e. travelling by night without consulting any one), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” [prev. cited]}}
**2** **make common, share**, κοινώσαντας τὴν δύναμιν κοινὰ καὶ τὰ ἀποβαίνοντα ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.39, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lg.”}}[same place]; variant for{ἐκοινώνησε} in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1264a1}}:—in _middle_, κοινάσομαι [ὕμνον] λύρᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.”}}[same place]: _aorist_ _middle_ in _active_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Jusj.”}}; κ. τὴν οὐσίαν τῇ τοῦ παιδός **unite** one to the other, Is. [prev. cited]
**3** **make common, defile**, τὸν ἄνθρωπον {{LXX+NT|NT.Matt.15.11; γαστέρα μιαροφαγίᾳ LXX.4Mac.7.6}}:— _middle_, **deem profane,** {{NT|NT.Acts.10.15.}}
**II** _middle_, with _accusative_, **undertake together, make common cause in**, βουλεύματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1347; κοινούμεθα.. ἐγώ τε καὶ Λάχης τὸν λόγον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 196c; τὸ πρᾶγμα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.30; κοινουμένη τὰς ξυμφοράς σοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 608, compare 858; κοινοῦσθαι τὸν στόλον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.8; τὴν τύχην 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Vectigalibus” 4.32.}}
**II.2** **take counsel with, consult**, especially an oracle or god, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.2.15, variant in “HG” 7.1.27: generally, πρός τινας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 930c; περὶ πάντων ἑαυτοῖς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 7.16.3; τοῖς ἰατροῖς περί τινων 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ ἐθῶν - Scripta Minora” 5; τοῖς φίλοις περὶ τὸ πρακτέον 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.8.1}}; ὧν ἄν τις κοινώσαιτο δόξαις **agree with**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 993b12: _absolutely_, οὔτ᾽ ἠθέλησας οὔτ᾽ ἐγὼ 'κοινωσάμην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 539}}; simply, **communicate**, τὰ κατ᾽ ἐμὲ τῇ βουλῇ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.72; μηδὲν τῇ γυναικὶ χρήσιμον 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 361.}}
**II.3** c._genitive_, **to be partner** or **partaker**, τινος of a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1709, “Cyc.” 634, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.93, etc.}}; τινί τινος with one in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 933.}}
**II.4** **come to terms**, μοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 218e.}}
**III** _passive_, **have communication with**, λέχει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 38, compare 217: _metaphorically_, ἀλλήλοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 673d}}; ξανθῷ χρώματι -ωθέν, i.e. **tinged with** yellow, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 59b.}}

2841:
** κοινων-έω**,
_future_ -ήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 54o c}}: _perfect_ κεκοινώνηκα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 246d, etc.:}} —_passive_, _future_ κοινωνήσομαι (see. below): _perfect_ κεκοινώνημαι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 801e}}:—**have** or **do in common with, share, take part in** a thing **with** another, with _genitive_ _of things_ et _dative_ _person_, τῆς πολιτείας κ. τινί {{Refs|[prev. work] 753a}}; κ. πόνων καὶ κινδύνων ἀλλήλοις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 686a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.21}}; κ. αὐτοῖς ὧν ἔπραττον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 6.3.1; σιτήσεώς τισι 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.101}}: also in _active_ sense, **give a share of**.., βρωτοῦ μηδενὸς μηδένα τούτῳ κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.61}}; τὰ περὶ τὰς κτήσεις τοῖς συσσιτίοις ὁ νομοθέτης ἐκοινώνησε (variant{ἐκοίνωσε}) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1264a1; πυρὸς ἢ ὕδατος κ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 46; πάντων ἐκοινώνει μοι τῶν ἀπορρήτων [prev. author] “Philops.” 34.}}
**2** κ. τινός **have a share of, take part in** a thing, χθονός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 325; μύθου [prev. author] “Ch.” 165; κακῶν [prev. author] “Th.” 1038; γάμων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 546; τάφου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1055; τύχης [prev. author] “Med.” 302; σίτου καὶ ποτοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.22; τῆς πολιτείας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1268a18, etc.}}; τῶν αὐτῶν κ. πάντων **share** all things **in common**,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1257a22; ἱερῶν “SIG” 1106.7 (Cos, 4th-3rd c.BC)}}; θυσίας {{Refs|“Inscription Magn.” 44.19 (Decrees Corc.)}}; ἦθος παιδείας κεκοινωνηκός Aristeas 290; φύσεως κεκοινώνηκε σαρκίνης {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 27}}; πάθους, of infection, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.312.}}
**2.b** of **partnership** in business, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 969.13 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**3** κ. τινί **go shares with, have dealings with** a man, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 692, “Av.” 653, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 343d, etc.}}; also of things, κοινωνεῖν μὲν ἡγοῦμαι καὶ τοῦτο τοῖς πεπολιτευμένοις I think that this also **is concerned with** my public measures, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.58}}; στολὴν φοινικίδα.. ἥκιστα.. γυναικείᾳ κ. **has** least **in common with**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 11.3; οὐδὲν τραγῳδίᾳ κ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1453b10, compare “SE” 179b16}}: in Medicine texts, **sympathize**, of bodily parts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.38:}} —_passive_, ἐγκώμια κεκοινωνημένα εὐχαῖς **united with**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 801e.}}
**4** with Preps., φύσις ἡ θήλεια τῇ τοῦ ἄρρενος γένους κ. εἰς ἅπαντα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 453a; κ. περί τινος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.18.6.}}
**5** c._accusative_ _cognate_, κ. κοινωνίαν τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 881e; κ. ἴσα πάντα τοῖς ἀνδράσι [prev. author] “R.” 540c:}} rarely with _accusative_ _of things_, κ. φόνον τινί **commit** murder **in common with** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1048.}}
**6** _absolutely_, **share in an opinion, agree**, σκόπει.., πότερον κοινωνεῖς καὶ ξυνδοκεῖ σοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 49d.}}
**7** **communicate, join**, ἡ ἐρυθρὰ θάλασσα κ. πρὸς τὴν ἔξω.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 354a2.}}
**8** **form a community**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1280a26, etc.}}
**II** of sexual intercourse, κ. γυναικί, ἀνδρί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 784e, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 1.2, 10.2, “PFlor.” 36.6 (4th c.AD)}}:—_passive_, ὑπὸ μηδενός ποτε κοινωνήσεται εἰ μὴ ὑπὸ σοῦ μόνου {{Refs|“PMag.Osl.” 1.293.}}

2842:
** κοινων-ία**, ἡ,
**communion, association, partnership**, κ. μαλθακά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.97; οὔτε φιλία ἰδιώταις οὔτε κ. πόλεσιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.10; ὅτῳ δὲ μὴ ἔνι κ., φιλία οὐκ ἂν εἴη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 507e; ἐν ταῖς κ. τε καὶ ὁμιλίαις [prev. author] “Lg.” 861e, compare “Smp.” 182c}}; ἡ περὶ.. ἀνθρώπους πρὸς ἀλλήλους κ.{{Refs|[prev. work] 188c; ἐν διαλύσει τῆς κ. [prev. author] “R.” 343d; ἡ τῶν γυναικῶν κ. τοῖς ἀνδράσι, i.e. co-education, [prev. work] 466c}}; ἀνθρωπίνη κ. human **society**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Plt.” 276b; ἡ κ. ἡ πολιτική 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1252a7; αὕτη ἡ κ., of marriage, [prev. work] 1334b33; πόλις ἡ γενῶν καὶ κωμῶν κ. [prev. work] 1281a1}}; _fellowship, _{{NT|NT.Acts.2.42, +others}}; ἡ πρὸς τὸν Δία κ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.19.27.}}
**b** **joint-ownership,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PLond. 2.311.2 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**2** with _genitive_ objecti, λυγραὶ.. τῶν ὅπλων κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1377; γάμων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 721a; γυναικῶν [prev. author] “R.” 461e}}; ἡ ἡδονῆς τε καὶ λύπης κ. συνδεῖ{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 462b; τῶν πόνων [prev. author] “Ti.” 87e; βοηθείας καὶ φιλίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.28}}; βίου, of marriage, {{NT+6th c.AD+|“BGU” 1051.9 (Aug.); ἡ κ. τοῦ ἁγίου πνεύματος NT.2Cor.13.14 (later, of Holy “Communion”, 6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 7.11); κ. τῶν ἱερῶν “Supp.epigram” 4.247 (from Panamara)}}; τίς θαλάσσης βουκόλοις κ.; what have herdsmen **to do with** the sea? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 254}}; τίς δαὶ κατόπτρου καὶ ξίφους κ.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 140; λύπη μανίας κοινωνίαν ἔχει τινά 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 296}}; opposed to ἀκοινωνησία, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 423.}}
**II** **sexual intercourse**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1276; γυναικὸς λαμβάνειν κοινωνίαν 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 20.3.}}
**III** **charitable contribution, alms,** {{NT|NT.Rom.15.26, NT.Heb.13.16, “Jahresh.” 4 “Beibl.” 37.}}
**III.2** **charitable disposition**, opposed to πλεονεξία, {{Refs|“Corpus Hermeticum” 13.9.}}
**IV** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus name for 2, “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 8.}}

2843:
** κοινων-ικός**, ή, όν,
**held in common**, τὰ κ. property **held by corporations**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 14.16, compare “BCH” 50.16 (Delph., 4th c.BC, probably)}}; κ. ἐλαιών {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 1037.14 (1st c.AD),compare “PGiss.” 30.7 (2nd c.AD).}}
**b** **relating to partnerships**, [δίκαι] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 52.2.}}
**c** κοινωνικά, τά, **tax on corporations,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 5.59 (2nd c.BC), 100.10 (2nd c.BC).}}
**2** **social**, ἰσότης κοινωνική [ἡ δικαιοσύνη] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Definitiones” 411e; κ. ἀρετή 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1283a38}}; [φιλίαι] {{Refs|[prev. author] “EN” 1161b14.}}
**3** **sociable**, κ. καὶ φιλικὴ διάθεσις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.44.1, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.43d; φύσει ἐσμὲν κ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 525, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.13.5}}: _superlative_, τὸν ἄνθρωπον ἡ φύσις κατεσκεύασε -ώτατον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus “Fragmenta” 71}} H.; τὸ -κόν **sociability**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 2.8.3.}}
**3.b** of certain signs of the zodiac, {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 1.166.}}
**4** **giving a share of**, τῶν ὄντων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 56: _absolutely_, κ. ὁ Ἑρμῆς}} **ready to share luck with others**, proverbial in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1401a20}}; **liberal**, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.1Tim.6.18, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 69}}; opposed to φθονερός, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 4.817.}}
**5** with _dative_, **in communion** with, τῇ ἐκκλησίᾳ {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 8 “Jusj.”}}
**II** _active_, **receptive, sharing in**, φωτός {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.36}}.
**III** _adverb_ -κῶς, χρῆσθαι τοῖς εὐτυχήμασι to suffer others **to partake in** one's good fortune, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.48.7; κ. βιῶναι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.9; ζῆν κ. καὶ φιλικῶς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1108c, etc.}}
**III.2** in Medicine texts, **by sympathy**, κ. σπᾶσθαι probably in {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 3.140.}}

2844:
** κοινων-ός**, ὁ,
also ἡ, **companion, partner**, τινος of or in a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1037, 1352, “Supp.” 344, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 499; τῆς ἐπιβουλῆς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.68; ἱερῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 868e; τῆς ἀρχῆς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.63, 8.46}}; ὁ τοῦ κακοῦ κ. **accomplice** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 730; ἀνοσίων αὐτοῖς ἔργων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 325a; κ. περὶ νόμων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 810c}}; τινι in a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 637: with _dative_ _person_, κ. ἀλλήλοις τῶν τιμῶν with each other, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.24.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **partner, fellow**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 284, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 333b, “Phdr.” 239c, etc.; ὁ σὸς κ., οὐχ ὁ ἐμός 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.21; ἴσοι καὶ κ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1133b3}}; κοινωνοὶ λιμένων, of a **societas publicanorum** which farmed harbour-dues, {{Refs|“BCH” 10.267 (from Syme)}}; of **joint-owners,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PAmh. 2.100 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**3** **familiar spirit**, {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.17.11.}}
**II** as _adjective_, ={κοινός},{ξίφος} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1173.}}

2845:
** κοίτ-η**, ἡ,
(κεῖμαι) ={κοῖτος} 1, once in [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.341 (variant{οἴκῳ})}}]; **bedstead,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.330.16, +others, Wilcken “Chr.” 244.3 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; especially **marriage-bed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 804 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 17; οὐ γὰρ ἐκ μιᾶς κ. ἔβλαστον [prev. author] “fragment” 546; τᾶς ἀπλήστου κ. ἔρος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 152 (Lyric poetry), etc.; ἀνάνδρου κοίτας λέκτρον [prev. work] 436 (Lyric poetry)}}; also πετρίνη κοίτη, of a cave, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 160 (_anapaest meter_)}}; τειρομέναν νοσερᾷ κ. on a sick-**bed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 132 (Lyric poetry)}}; κοίταν δ᾽ ἔχει νέρθεν, of one dead, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1706 (Lyric poetry); κ. σκληρά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 942d, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.1: _plural_, ἔννυχοι κ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 11.25; νυμφίδιοι κ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 249 (Lyric poetry)}}: _metaphorically_, of the sea, ἐν μεσημβριναῖς κοίταις.. εὕδοι πεσών {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 566}}; of the **bed** of a river, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Aedificiis” 5.5, 6th c.AD: Philoponus, Joannes Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 586.21, 6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “de Mensibus” 4.10.}}
**2** **lair** of a wild beast, **nest** of a bird, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 155 (Lyric poetry); χελιδόνων 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 16.15}}; κ. ποιεῖσθαι, of the spider, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 623a12}}; of the fish ἐξώκοιτος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 171.1.}}
**3** **quarters**, τῶν φυλακιτῶν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“BGU” 1007.14 (3rd c.BC),compare “PTeb.” 179 (2nd c.BC)}}; see infr. VI.
**4** **pen, fold** for cattle, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PLips.” 118.15 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **act of going to bed**, τῆς κοίτης ὥρη **bed**-time, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.10, 5.20}}; τραπέζῃ καὶ κοίτῃ δέκεσθαι to entertain 'at **bed** and board', {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; τὴν σκηνὴν εἰς κ. διέλυον for **going to bed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.1 (but κεῖσθαι κοίταν}} **to lie still** in death, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1494 (Lyric poetry)).}}
**III** **lodging, entertainment,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|PTeb. 122.1 (1st c.BC), +others}}
**IV** of **sexual connexion**, κ. διδόναι {{LXX|LXX.Num.5.20, cf. LXX.Lev.18.20; κ. σπέρματος [prev. work] 15.16}}; κ. ἔχειν ἐκ.. to become pregnant by a man, {{NT|NT.Rom.9.10}}; in bad sense, **lasciviousness**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 13.13 (_plural_).}}
**V** **parcel, lot** of land, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PAmh.” 2.88.9 (2nd c.AD), “PRyl.” 168.9 (2nd c.AD).}}
**VI** **chest, case**, or **basket**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 122, 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 76, “IG” 22.120.37,40, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 129.2, “PPetr.” 2p.10 (3rd c.BC, unless in signification 1.3), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ep.Sat.” 21; αἱ μυστικαὶ κ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 28.}}

2846:
** κοιτών**, ῶνος, ὁ,
**bed-chamber**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 6, “PTeb.” 120.14 (1st c.BC), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.69, etc.; ὁ ἐπὶ τοῦ κοιτῶνος}}**chamberlain,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Acts.12.20, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.22.15}}; ἐπὶ κ. Σεβαστοῦ, = Latin _cubicularius Augusti_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2947 (Caria, 2nd c.AD),compare “IG” 14.2143, +others: rejected by the Atticists, who hold δωμάτιον to be correct,compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.79, 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 227.}}
**2** **grave,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.464 (from Catana).}}
**3** **nursery**, ἐν κ. εἶναι to be an infant, minor, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 155 “Praef.”}}
**II** **landing-place,** {{Refs|Stad. 128.}}

2847:
** κόκκ-ῐνος**, η, ον,
**scarlet**, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.19, NT.Heb.9.19, “PHamb.” 10.24 (2nd c.AD), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 15; κ. γενόμενος}}**blushing,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Comica Adespota 19.3 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**II** _substantive_ κόκκινα, τά, **scarlet clothes**, ἐν κ. περιπατεῖν, κ. φορεῖν, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.22.10, 4.11.34; - ων βαφαί “PHolm.” 21.41: _sing._, LXX.Exo.25.4.}}

2848:
** κόκκος**, ὁ,
**grain, seed**, as of the pomegranate, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 372, 412, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.143, 5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 36, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.37, “PTeb.” 273.47 (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}; κ. Βαβυλώνιος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Epistulae” 54}}; of the poppy, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euphro Comicus 11.11}}; of the pine, {{Refs|“IG” 14.966.12}}; of wheat, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 3.3}}; of weeds in corn, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PLond.” 5.1697.13 (6th c.AD)}}; compare Κνίδιος: _metaphorically_, νόου δέ μοι οὐκ ἔνι κ. not **a grain** of sense, {{Refs|Timo 66.3.}}
**2** a measure, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.166, 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “fragment” 35.}}
**II** '**berry' (gall) of kermes oak**, used to dye scarlet, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.7.3, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.32}}: hence, **scarlet** (the colour), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Dromo Comicus 1.4, “PHolm.” 22.1.}}
**II.2** κ. or κ. βαφική, ἡ, **kermes oak, Quercus coccifera**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.48, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.36.1.}}
**III** **pill**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Alexander Trallianus Medicus 5.4.}}
**IV** in _plural_, **testicles,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 12.222 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus).}}
**IV.2** **pudenda muliebria**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

2849:
** κολάζω**,
_future_ κολάσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.136, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 714d, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐκόλασα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 927, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.40}}:—_middle_, _future_ κολάσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 27, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.19}}; twice _contraction_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus}}, _2nd pers._ _singular_ κολᾷ {{Refs|“Eq.” 456}}, _participle_ κολωμένους {{Refs|“V.” 244}}: _aorist_ ἐκολασάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.78, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 240d}}:—_passive_, _future_ -ασθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.87, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐκολάσθην {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.68}}: _perfect_ κεκόλασμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.8, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.139}}:—**check, chastise**, τὰς ἐπιθυμίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 491e; τὸ πλεονάζον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.663e, etc.; τὴν ἀμετρίαν 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.29}}:—_passive_, **to be corrected**, τὸ ἐν μέλιτι χολῶδες -άζεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 59, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 20.12}}: _perfect_ _participle_ _passive_, **chastened**, εὐπειθὲς καὶ κεκολ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1119b12; δίαιτα 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 86}}; ῥήτωρ κεκ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 6.149; ἰσχὺς κ. ἐς ῥυθμούς 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.17.3}}; also of an athlete, ἀπέριττος τὰ μυώδη καὶ μὴ κεκ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Gym.” 31.}}
**2** **chastise, punish**, τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1322, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 7, etc.}}; τὰ σέμν᾽ ἔπη κόλαζ᾽ ἐκείνους **use** your proud words **in reproving** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1108: with _dative_ modi, λόγοις κ. τινά [prev. work] 1160; θανάτῳ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1172, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 28.3}}; πληγαῖς, τιμωρίαις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 784d, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.50; ἀτιμίαις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 309a}}:—_middle_, **get** a person **punished**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 406, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 324c, variant 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.7}}:—_passive_, **to be punished**, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.1, etc.}}; of divine **retribution**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.566e}}; **suffer injury**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 3.24.}}
**3** of a drastic method of checking the growth of the almond-tree, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.7.6}}:—_passive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “CP” 1.18.9; compare κόλασις 1.}}
**4** _passive_ with _genitive_, **to be badly in need of,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PFay. 120.5 (1st-2nd c.AD), compare 115.19 (2nd c.AD), “BGU” 249.4 (2nd c.AD).}}

2851:
** κόλ-ᾰσις**, εως, ἡ,
**checking the growth** of trees, especially almond-trees, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.18.2 (_plural_).}}
**2** **chastisement, correction**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “παραγγελίαι” 5, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 26a, +others, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.41}}; opposed to τιμωρία, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1369b13}}; of divine **retribution,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Matt.25.46, +others: _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 323e, +others, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.52 W.}}

2853:
** κολλ-άω**,
(κόλλα) **glue, cement**, τι περὶ τὸν τράχηλον, τι πρός τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 75d, 82d; ἐπιστύλια ἐπὶ τοὺς κίονας “IG” 22.1668.46}}; **mend** a broken vessel, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work]11(2).161 A 111 (Delos, 3rd c.BC), “POxy.” 1449.15 (3rd c.AD).}}
**2** **join** one metal or other substance to another, κ. χρυσὸν ἔν τε λευκὸν ἐλέφαντα, i.e. **make** [a crown] **inlaid with** gold and ivory, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.78:}} —_passive_, κολλώμενα **glued together**, opposed to γομφούμενα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 463}}; ὁ κολλώμενος σίδηρος **welded** iron, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.619a; στραγγαλὶς χρυσᾶ κεκολλημένη “POxy.” 1449.23 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II** generally, **join fast together, unite**, ἄλφιτον ὕδατι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 34}}; χαλκὸν ἐπ᾽ ἀνέρι κολλᾶν, of one applying a cupping-glass, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Cleobulina Lyrica 1, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “Θρασύβουλος - Scripta Minora” 23}}; **close up** wounds, {{Refs|[prev. author] 11.440:}} —_passive_, κολλέεσθαι, of poison entering the system, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 19 (“Hermes” 53.66)}}; κολλᾷ καὶ συνδεῖ πάντα ἤθη [ὁ πόθος] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 776a}}:—_passive_, **cleave to**, κεκόλληται γένος πρὸς ἄτᾳ **is indissolubly bound** to.. (Blomf. for προσάψαι), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1566 (Lyric poetry); λόγος εἰς τὰ σπλάγχνα κολληθείς 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 113.4}}; of persons, κ. τινί {{NT|NT.Act.5.13}}; of things, ὁ κονιορτὸς ὁ κολληθείς τινι {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Luke.10.11: obscene meaning, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.73 (1st c.AD: Nicarchus Epigrammaticus).}}
**III** **put together, build**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 5.13}}:— _middle_, **fit together**, τροχάλεια {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 530.}}

2856:
** κολοβ-όω**,
**dock, curtail, mutilate**, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 101, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.80.13; τὰς χεῖρας καὶ τοὺς πόδας LXX.2Ki.4.12}}:—_passive_, **to be mutilated, imperfect**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Araros Comicus 3, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.6.3; τῆς γυναικὸς τὴν ῥῖνα κολοβοῦσθαι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.78; τῇ φώκῃ κεκολοβωμένοι πόδες 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 487b23, compare “GA” 771a2: with _genitive_, κεκολοβῶσθαι τῶν ἐκτὸς μορίων [prev. author] “PA” 695b2.}}
**II** of Time, **curtail, shorten**, τὰς ἡμέρας {{NT|NT.Mark.13.20, cf. NT.Matt.24.22 (_passive_).}}

2859:
** κόλπος**, ὁ,
**bosom, lap**, παῖδ᾽ ἐπὶ κόλπῳ ἔχουσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.400; ἂψ ὁ πάϊς πρὸς κόλπον ἐκλίνθη [prev. work] 467; ἡ δ᾽ ἄρα μιν κηώδεϊ δέξατο κόλπῳ (compare 111.1) [prev. work] 483}}; ἱμάντα τέῳ ἐγκάτθεο κ. put the girdle in thy **bosom**,{{NT+6th c.BC+|14.219; εἰς κόλπον πτύσαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 16.15 (compare πτύω); ἐν κόλπῳ εἶχες ὄφιν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 602; ὁ κ. Αβραάμ NT.Luke.16.22}}; frequently of pet birds or animals, τρέφειν ἐν κ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.102}}; κυνίδιον ἐν κόλπῳ τιθηνούμενον **lap**-dog, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.472c; κίσσαν ἐκ μέσων τῶν κόλπων ἁρπάσας 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Jud.Voc.” 8; so τὸ θυγάτριον ἐκ κόλπων τῶν ἐμῶν ἀναρπάσαντα 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 4.14: _metaphorically_, εἰς τοὺς εὐανθεῖς κ. λειμώνων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 373 (Lyric poetry); λειμώνων φύλλων τ᾽ ἐν κόλποις ναίω [prev. author] “Av.” 1094 (Lyric poetry)}}; also τὰ ὑπὸ κόλπου, ={τὰ ἀφροδίσια}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 39.}}
**2** = {αἰδοῖον γυναικεῖον}, especially **vagina**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.16, +others, 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 196, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.222: _plural_, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.70b, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 5.62.}}
**2.b** κόλποι τῆς ὑστέρας supposed **sinuses** in the womb, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 31, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.9 (_sing._), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 14.4.}}
**2.c** in poets more vaguely of the whole **sinus genitalis, womb**, in _plural_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1145 (Lyric poetry), 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Jovem” 15: _sing._, [prev. author] “Del.” 214; δεσποίνας ὑπὸ κόλπον ἔδυν 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 32c. 8; θεὸς διὰ κόλπου [prev. work] 31i24}}: _metaphorically_, of the grave, σῶμα σὸν ἐν κόλποις.. γαῖα καλύπτει {{Refs|“IG” 2.3839, compare 3412, “epigram Gr.” 214.7 (from Rhenea)}}; κ. ἡμερῶν, of the **womb** of time, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Ezekiel Poeta Judaeus “Exagoge - Mnemosyne” 39.}}
**2.d** of other cavities, οἱ κ. τῆς κοιλίας, in the ἐχῖνος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 530b27}}; of the **ventricles** of the heart, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.216.}}
**II** **fold of a garment**, especially as it fell over the girdle, frequently in _plural_, δεύοντο δὲ δάκρυσι κ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.570, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 539 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.: also in _singular_, κ. βαθὺν καταλιπόμενος τοῦ κιθῶνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.125}}; κόλπον ἀνιεμένη letting down the **bosom of her robe**, i.e. baring her breast, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.80; ἐπὶ σφυρὰ κόλπον ἀνεῖσαι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.134}}; κρύψε δὲ παρθενίαν ὠδῖνα κόλποις, i.e. she concealed her pregnancy **by the loose folds of her robe**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.31; κατακρύψασ᾽ ὑπὸ κόλπῳ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.469; κόλπῳ φέρουσα.. πεπλώματος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1044}}; ὑπὸ κόλπου (variant -ῳ) χεῖρας ἔχειν 'keep one's hand in one's **pocket**', of a stingy person, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.16; ὑπὸ κόλπου 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 37, 81, “Hes.” 2, “Merc.Cond.” 27}}; ὑπὸ κόλπον [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {μασχαλοληπτεῖ}, variant in 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 12.}}]
**III** any **bosom-like hollow**:
**III.1** of the sea, first in a half-literal sense, of a sea-goddess, Θέτις δ᾽ ὑπεδέξατο κόλπῳ received him **in her bosom**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.136, compare above 1.1: generally, δῦτε θαλάσσης εὐρέα κ. 18.140, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.435; εἴσω ἁλὸς εὐρέα κ. lines 21.125: in _plural_, κατὰ δεινοὺς κ. ἁλός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.52; also κόλποι αἰθέρος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.88; Ἐρέβους ἐν ἀπείροσι κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 694.}}
**III.2** **bay, gulf**, Ἑρμιόνην Ἀσίνην τε, βαθὺν κατὰ κ. ἐχούσας, i.e. βαθὺν κατεχούσας κόλπον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.560; Μηλιεὺς κ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 486}}; κ. Ῥέας, i.e. the Adriatic, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pr.” 837; Τυρσηνικὸς κ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 598, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.11, 7.58, 198, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.90, etc.}}
**III.3** **vale**, κ. Ἀργεῖος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.49; Νεμέας [prev. author] “O.” 9.87, compare 14.23; Ἐλευσινίας Δηοῦς ἐν κόλποις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1121 (Lyric poetry); κ. Τροίας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 130 (Lyric poetry); Πιερικὸς κ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.99, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.17.}}
**III.4** of a fortified site, **salient**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 86.8.}}
**III.5** ὁ κ. τοῦ ἅρματος **bottom** of the chariot, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.22.35.}}
**III.6** **fistulous ulcer** which spreads under the skin, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.128, 1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 44.8.22, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.125.}}
**IV** in Tactics, **enveloping force**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 21.5.}}

2860:
** κολυμβ-άω**
(_Doric dialect_ -φάω _accusative_ to {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 526.2)}}, **dive, plunge headlong**, εἰς τὸν Τάρταρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 108.21; εἰς τὰ φρέατα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 350a, compare “La.” 193c, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.44, etc.; εἰς κολυμβήθραν μύρου 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 300.}}
**2** **swim**, τοὺς δυναμένους κολυμβᾶν {{NT|NT.Act.27.43, compare “Hippiatrica” 26.}}

2861:
** κολυμβ-ήθρα**, ἡ,
**place for diving, swimming-bath**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 453d, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.25; κ. μύρου 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 300.}}
**II** **wine-vat, tun**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.83.}}
**III** **reservoir, cistern**, {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.18.17.}}
**IV** **baptismal font,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|POxy. 147 (6th c.AD).}}

2863:
** κομάω**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κομ-έω**, (κόμη) **let the hair grow long**, Ἄβαντες ὄπιθεν κομόωντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.542; κάρη κομόωντες Ἀχαιοί 3.43, +others; κ. τὴν κεφαλήν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.168; τὰ ὀπίσω κ. τῆς κεφαλῆς [prev. work] 180}}; τὰ ἐπὶ δεξιὰ τῶν κεφαλέων κ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 191; τὸ γένειον τῇ κεφαλῇ ὁμοίως κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 4.28; ξανθοτάτοις βοτρύχοισι κ. 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 189; ἄρσεσιν οὐκ ἐπέοικε κ. 1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 212; Λακεδαιμόνιοι.. οὐ γὰρ κομῶντες πρὸ τούτου ἀπὸ τούτου κομᾶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1367a29, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.15; ἐλακωνομάνουν ἅπαντες.., ἐκόμων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1282}}; μὴ φθονεῖθ᾽ ἡμῖν (i.e. τοῖς ἱππεῦσι) κομῶσι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eq.” 580; κομῶν καὶ αὐχμηρός 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1413a9, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.26}}; ἔνορκον ἂν ποιησαίμην μὴ πρότερον κομήσειν (in token of a vow) πρίν.. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89c; ἀνὴρ μὲν ἐὰν κομᾷ, ἀτιμία αὐτῷ ἐστι· γυνὴ δὲ ἐὰν κομᾷ, δόξα αὐτῇ ἐστιν NT.1Cor.11.14 - 15.}}
**2** **plume oneself, give oneself airs**, τοιοῦτος ἀνὴρ ὢν ποιητὴς οὐ κομῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 545, compare “Pl.” 170}}; οὗτος ἐπὶ τυραννίδι ἐκόμησε **aimed at** the monarchy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.71}}; ἐπὶ τῷ κομᾷ; on what **do you plume yourself**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1317; μηδὲν ταύτῃ γε κομήσῃς [prev. author] “Pl.” 572; κ. ἐπὶ κάλλει 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 45, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 1}}; ἐπ᾽ Ἠρίννῃ κ., of her lover, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.322 (1st c.AD: Antiphanes Macedo Epigrammaticus): with _dative_, 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 3.192.}}
**II** of horses, χρυσέῃσιν ἐθείρῃσιν κομόωντε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.42, 13.24.}}
**III** of the hair itself, **to be long**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 3.28.}}
**IV** _metaphorically_, of trees, plants, etc., [οὖθαρ ἀρούρης] μέλλεν ἄφαρ ταναοῖσι κομήσειν ἀσταχύεσσιν soon were the fields **to wave** with long ears, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 454; μᾶζαι βώλοις κομῶσαι 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 165; ἁ δὲ καλὰ νάρκισσος ἐπ᾽ ἀρκεύθοισι κομάσαι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.133, compare 4.57; αἴγειρος φύλλοισι κομόωσα 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 3.928; ὄρος κεκομημένον ὕλῃ 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Dianam” 41; ἡ γῆ φυτοῖς κομῶσα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 397a24, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Fragmenta” 75; κομῶντα λήϊα 5th-6th c.AD: Procopius Gazaeus Rhetor “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 23.}}
**V** ἀστέρες κομόωντες, ={κομῆται}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 1092.}}

2864:
** κόμ-η**, ἡ,
**hair of the head**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.406, etc.: less frequently in _plural_, κὰδ δὲ κάρητος οὔλας ἧκε κόμας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.231}}; κόμαι Χαρίτεσσιν ὁμοῖαι (i.e. κόμαις Χαρίτων) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.51}}; κόμην κείρειν, κείρεσθαι (see. κείρω); κόμην τρέφειν to let **the hair** grow long, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82; κ. φορεῖν “PGnom.” 188 (2nd c.AD)}}; κόμη δι᾽ αὔρας ἀκτένιστος ᾄσσεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1261; καθεῖσαν εἰς ὤμους κόμας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 695}}; κόμαι πρόσθετοι false **hair, wig**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.2, etc.}}; δοῦλος ὢν κόμην ἔχει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 911}}; κόμης ἀνάπλεως **unkempt**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 30.}}
**2** of the **beard**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.8.4.}}
**3** **gill** or **branchia** of the cuttlefish, uncertain in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 550b18}}: _plural_, **arms** or **suckers**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Maximus Tyrius Sophista 4.5.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **foliage** of trees, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.195, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 296, etc.; δόνακος 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 4.28}}; of herbs, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.164.7, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.268}}; of corn, ληΐου κ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 88.3; λειμώνων κόμαι “IG” 14.1389i i 11}}; especially ={τραγοπώγων}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.7.1, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.143.}}
**III** **luminous tail of a comet**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 343a1, 346a15.}}

2865:
** κομίζω**,
_future_ κομιῶ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.546, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.γ, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 800, etc.}}; κομίσω only late, as {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.41 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus):}}_aorist_ ἐκόμισα, _Epic dialect_ ἐκόμισσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.579, κόμισσα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.322, κόμισα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.196}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐκόμιξα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.159}}: _perfect_ κεκόμικα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.115, etc.}}:—_middle_, _future_ κομιοῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 690, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.113, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ -ιεῦμαι, see below {{Refs|11.4}}; late κομίσομαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 135}}: _aorist_ ἐκομισάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.118, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ ἐκομισς- or κομισς-, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.316, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.284}}:—_passive_, _future_ -ισθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.52, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.301}}: _aorist_ ἐκομίσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.3, etc.}}: _perfect_ κεκόμισμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.241: but more frequently in middle sense, see below 11.2}}: (κομέω):—**take care of, provide for**, τόν γε γηράσκοντα κομίζω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.541; τόνδε τ᾽ ἐγὼ κομιῶ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.546; ἐμὲ κεῖνος ἐνδυκέως ἐκόμιζε 17.113, etc.; κόμισσε δὲ Πηνελόπεια, παῖδα δὲ ὢς ἀτίταλλε 18.322, compare 20.68: rare in Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 262, 344}}; **receive, treat**, φιλίως, οὐ πολεμίως κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.65}} codices:—more frequently in _middle_, καί σε.. κομίσσατο ᾧ ἐνὶ οἴκῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.284, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.316; Σίντιες.. ἄφαρ κομίσαντο πεσόντα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.594; κομίζεσθαί τινα ἐς τὴν οἰκίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.127, compare 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.15:}} —_passive_, οὔ τι κομιζόμενός γε θάμιζεν not often was he **attended to**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.451.}}
**2** of things, **attend, give heed to**, τὰ ο᾽ αὐτῆς ἔργα κόμιζε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.490, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.350; κτήματα μὲν.. κομιζέμεν ἐν μεγάροισι 23.355; δῶμα κ., of the mistress of the house, 16.74}}, etc.; τὸν χρυσόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.153}}; ἔξω κ. πηλοῦ πόδα **keep** it out of the mud, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 697}}:—_middle_, ἔργα κ. Δημήτερος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 393}}; Δημήτερος ἱερὸν ἀκτὴν μέτρῳ εὖ κομίσασθαι ἐν ἄγγεσιν **store up**.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 600.}}
**II** **carry away so as to preserve**, Ἀμφίμαχον.. κόμισαν μετὰ λαὸν Ἀχαιῶν **they carried away** his body, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.196 (so in _middle_, κόμισαί με}} **carry** me **safe away**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5.359, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 774)}}; of things, τὴν δὲ κόμισσε κῆρυξ the herald **took up** the mantle, that it might not be lost, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.183; [τρυφάλειαν] κόμισαν.. ἑταῖροι 3.378, compare 13.579}}; later, simply, **save, rescue**, ἄνδρ᾽ ἐκ θανάτου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.56; ἄρουραν πατρίαν σφίσιν κόμισον [prev. author] “O.” 2.14}}; of the dead, νεκρὸν κ. **carry out to burial**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1264, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1397}}:—in _middle_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.21}}; also, simply, **carry** the body **home**, opposed to θάπτω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 683, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.71.}}
**II.2** **carry off as a prize** or **booty**, χρυσὸν δ᾽ Ἀχιλεὺς ἐκόμισσε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.875; κόμισσα δὲ μώνυχας ἵππους 11.738}}; τέσσαρας ἐξ ἀέθλων νίκας ἐκόμιξαν four victories **they won**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 2.19; ἔπαινος, ὃν κομίζετον τοῦδ᾽ ἀνδρός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1411}}:—in _middle_, Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.67}}:—later frequently, **get for oneself, acquire, gain**, δόξαν ἐσθλήν variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 432; τριώβολον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 690; τὴν ἀξίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 615c}}; τὰ ἆθλα αὐτῆς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 621d; κ. τί τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 580; τι παρά τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.43; τι ἀπό τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.10}}; **gather in, reap**, καρπόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.14:}}_perfect_ _passive_ in _middle_ sense, ὑμεῖς τοὺς καρποὺς κεκόμισθε you **have reaped** the fruits, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.231; κεκόμισται χάριν [prev. author] 21.171; ὡμολόγει κεκομίσθαι τὴν προῖκα [prev. author] 27.14, compare 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.22}}; simply, **receive**, ἐνηρόσιον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 1044.31 (Halic., 4th-3rd c.BC)}}; ἐπιστολήν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 186 (3rd c.BC)}}; μισθόν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).99.24 (Epid., 2nd c.BC)}}; ἀπ᾽ ἀλλήλων χρείας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3 “fragment” 84.}}
**II.3** **receive** a missile in one's body, ἀλλά τις Ἀργείων κόμισε χροΐ (i.e. τὸν ἄκοντα) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.456, compare 463:}} —_middle_, ὡς δή μιν σῷ ἐν χροΐ πᾶν κομίσαιο (i.e. τὸ ἔγχος) {{Refs|22.286.}}
**II.4** **carry, convey**, κόμισαν δέπας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|23.699, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.68, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.83, etc.}}; κομίζοις ἂν σεαυτόν **betake** thyself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 444}}:—_passive_, **to be conveyed, journey, travel**, by land or sea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.43, etc.}}; εἴσω κομίζου **get thee** in, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1035, compare “Pr.” 394}}; κ. παρά τινα **betake oneself** to him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.73}}: in this sense _future_ and _aorist_ _middle_ sometimes occur, κομιεύμεθα ἐς Σῖριν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.62; οἳ ἂν κομίσωνται.. ἐς Βαβυλῶνα [prev. author] 1.185; ἔξω κομίσασθ᾽ οἴκων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 167 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.5** **bring to** a place, **bring in, introduce**, κόμιζέ νύν μοι παῖδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 530}}; **import**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 370e, etc.; ξενικοῦ κομισθέντος νομίσματος [prev. author] “Lg.” 742c; κ. τὴν φιλοσοφίαν εἰς τοὺς Ἕλληνας 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.28; οἱ κομίσαντες τὴν δόξαν ταύτην 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1096a17, compare “Metaphorical” 990b2}}:—in _middle_, [τὸν ἀνδριάντα] ἐπὶ Δήλιον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.118; ποίμνας ἐς δόμους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 63, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 833.}}
**II.6** **conduct, escort**, τί μέλλεις κομίζειν δόμων τόνδ᾽ ἔσ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 678 (Lyric poetry), compare “Ph.” 841 (hexameters), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 113d, etc.}}; κ. ἐξ ὀμμάτων γυναῖκα τήνδε **take** her from my sight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1064; κ. ναῦς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.85; ἄρχοντα [prev. author] 8.61.}}
**II.7** **bring back** from exile, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.106 (uncertain)}}; τεὰν ψυχὰν κ. (from the world below), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “N.” 8.44; πάλιν κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 107e, etc.}}
**II.8** **get back, recover**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.59; τέκνων.. κομίσαι δέμας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 273 (hexameters), compare 495}}:—_middle_, **get back for oneself**, τὸν παῖδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ba.” 1225, compare “IT” 1362; τὴν βασιλείαν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 549; τοὺς ἄνδρας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.113, compare 4.117; τοὺς νεκροὺς ὑποσπόνδους κ. [prev. author] 6.103; τὰ πρέποντα [prev. author] 4.98; ἃ νῦν ἀπολαβεῖν οὐ δυνάμεθα διὰ πολέμου, ταῦτα διὰ πρεσβείας ῥᾳδίως κομιούμεθα 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.22}}; especially of money, **recover** debts, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.14, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.38, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.7, etc.; διπλάσια 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.57; τόκους πολλαπλασίους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 556a, etc.; κ. τιμωρίαν παρά τινος 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.70}}; κ. τὴν θυγατέρα **take back one's** daughter (on the death of her husband), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.8.}}
**II.9** _metaphorically_, **rescue from oblivion**, ἀοιδοὶ καὶ λόγοι τὰ καλὰ ἔργ᾽ ἐκόμισαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.30.}}
**II.10** **bring, give**, θράσος.. ἀνδράσι θνῄσκουσι κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 804 (_anapaest meter_):}} —_active_ and _middle_ combined, χθὼν πάντα κομίζει καὶ πάλιν κομίζεται **gives** all things and **gets** them **back** again, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 539, compare 89, 668.}}
**II** **cite** as an authority, Θεμιστοκλέα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.205 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**II.12** in Medicine texts, **extract, remove**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.632.}}
**III** _passive_, **come** or **go back, return**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.76, +others; ἐκομίσθησαν ἐπ᾽ οἴκου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.33, compare 73; κομισθεὶς οἴκαδε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 614b.}}

2867:
** κονι-άω**,
(κονία {{Refs|111)}} **plaster with lime** or **stucco**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.29, 23.208, “IG” 22.1672.107, 140, 179, “Inscription Magn.” 100b.40, etc.:}} —_middle_, κ. τοὺς ἐγχελεῶνας **have** them **plastered**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 592a4}}:—_passive_, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Bito Mechanicus 55.9, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Comp. Arist.Cat.Ma.” 4, “IG” 7.2712.35 (Acraeph.); τάφοι κεκονιαμένοι NT.Matt.23.27.}}
**2** generally, **daub over**, as with pitch, ἀγγεῖα κεκονιαμένα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.94.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, κ. τὸ πρόσωπον **paint, disguise** it, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Epistulae” 22}}:—_passive_, κεκονιαμένοι {{LXX|LXX.Pro.21.9 (κεκονιαμένους}} is _falsa lectio_ for{κεκονιμένους} {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 7.91d).}}

2868:
** κονῐορτ-ός**, ὁ,
(κόνις, ὄρνυμι) **dust raised** or **stirred up, cloud of dust**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.65; ὁ κ. δῆλος αὐτῶν ὡς ὁμοῦ προσκειμένων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 245, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.44}}; κ. τῆς ὕλης νεωστὶ κεκαυμένης, i.e. **a cloud** of woodashes,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 34; κ. καὶ ζάλη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 496d: in _plural_, Diocl.fragment 147.}}
**2** generally, **dirt, sweepings**, σαρώματα.. σὺν τῷ κ. Wilcken {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Chr.” 198.16 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **dirty fellow**, χαίρει τις αὐχμῶν ἢ ῥυπῶν, κ. ἀναπέφηνεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 34.6, compare 4th c.BC: Aristopho Comicus 10.8; Εὐκτήμων ὁ κ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.103.}}

2871:
** κοπή**, ἡ,
**cutting**, χόρτος εἰς κοπὴν καὶ ἐπινομήν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 499.15 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** **cutting in pieces, slaughter**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Josh.10.20, NT.Heb.7.1.}}
**3** κ. τριχός, tax levied on γερδιοραβδισταί, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“PAmh.” 2.119.4 (200 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator), compare “PFay.” 58.7 (2nd c.AD).}}
**4** **breaking up**, [νεφῶν] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 394a34.}}
**5** **pounding** in a mortar, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “Problemata - Physici et Medici Graeci Minores” 1.67.}}
**6** **dressing** of stone, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“CPHerm.” 127 (3rd c.AD).}}
**7** **striking, minting**, νομίσματος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Inscription Délos” 461 “Aa” 76 (2nd c.BC).}}
**8** **divorce**, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aquila LXX.Deu.24.3 (1).}}
**II** ={κόπος} 11, φλοίσβου μετὰ κοπήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 479 codices 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis (sed to be read κόπον).}}

2872:
** κοπ-ιάω**,
_future_ -άσω [ᾱ]: _aorist_ ἐκοπίᾱσα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Φάσμα” 36}}: _perfect_ κεκοπίᾱκα {{NT|NT.Rev.2.3}}: (κόπος):—**to be tired, grow weary**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 795, “fragment” 318.8, LXX.Deu.25.18, +others; κ. τὰ σκέλη 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 147}}, Men.[same place]; κ. ὑπὸ ἀγαθῶν **to be weary of** good things, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 735; ἐκ τῆς ὁδοιπορίας NT.John.4.6; τῇ διανοίᾳ 3rd c.BC: Erasistratus Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ ἐθῶν - Scripta Minora” 1}}: with _participle_, κ. ὀρχούμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 602; ζῶν “Anthologia Graeca” 12.46 (3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus); μὴ κοπιάτω φιλοσοφῶν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.59U., compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.185e:}}_aorist_ _participle_ κοπιάσας, **defunctus laboribus,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.1811}}:—_middle_ in _active_ sense, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 881a14.}}
**II** **work hard, toil,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Matt.6.28, etc.; μεθ᾽ ἡδονῆς κ. 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 266.6; εἴς τι NT.1Tim.4.10, cf. NT.Rom.16.6; ἔν τινι NT.1Tim.5.17; ἐπί τι LXX.Josh.24.13}}: with _infinitive_, **strive, struggle**, μὴ κοπία ζητεῖν {{Refs|“Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 37.7.}}
**III** ={κοπάζω}, **come to rest: arrive at a state of saturation,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PLeid.X. 30 (3rd-4th c.AD).}}

2873:
** κόπ-ος**, ὁ,
(κόπτω) **striking, beating**, ὀξύχειρι σὺν κόπῳ (Pauw for κτύπῳ) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 23 (Lyric poetry)}}; στέρνων κόπους (Seidler for κτύπους) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 794 (_anapaest meter_)}}; ={κοπανισμός}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II** **toil and trouble, suffering**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 210 (_plural_); ἀνδροδάϊκτος κόπος [prev. author] “fragment” 132a p. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1265}}; **pain** of a disease, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 880}}; κόπους παρέχειν τινί to give **trouble,** {{NT+2nd c.BC+|NT.Matt.26.10, +others, “PTeb.” 21.10 (2nd c.BC), “BGU” 844.10 (1st c.AD)}}; κόπον ἔχειν {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.62 K.; πάντα κ. ἀναδεξάμενος “SIG” 761 B 6 (Delph., 1st c.BC).}}
**II.2** **fatigue**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 21, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.190}}; κόπου ὕπο from **very weariness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 634; κόπῳ παρεῖμαι [prev. author] “Ph.” 852}}; κόπῳ δαμέντες, ἁλίσκεσθαι, {{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Rh.” 764, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.40; τῷ κ. ξυνεῖναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 321; τὰ γόνατα κ. ἕλοι μου [prev. author] “Lys.” 542: in _plural_, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 124; κόποι καὶ ὕπνοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 537b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 4.2, NT.2Cor.6.5, etc.}}; περὶ κόπων title of work by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus}}
**II.3** **work, exertion**, καμάραν ἀφ᾽ ἱδίων κόπων ἐποίησεν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“IG” 12(7).384 (from Amorgos), compare “BGU” 884.10 (1st c.AD)}}; κόπῳ κόπον λύειν proverbial in {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “Eup.” 1.2.8.}}

2874:
** κοπρ-ία**, ἡ,
(compare κόπερρα) **dunghill**, {{LXX+7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 7.6 (_plural_), 5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 43, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 845a5 (_plural_), LXX.Job.2.8, 3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.634, etc.}}; in Egypt, **rubbish-heap,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|PRyl. 2.162.17 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.; ἀναιρεῖσθαι ἀπὸ κοπρίας, of foundlings, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PGnom.” 238, compare 115 (2nd c.AD), “POxy.” 37i7 (1st c.AD).}}
**II** **refuse**, ἐν σείσματι κοσκίνου διαμένει κ. {{LXX|LXX.Sir.27.4}}; **manure,** [{{NT|NT.Luke.13.8 (variant{κόπρια}).}}]

2875:
** κόπτω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.28, etc.}}: _future_ κόψω {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 83, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 64, etc.:}}_aorist_ ἔκοψα, _Epic dialect_ κόψα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.203:}}_perfect_ κέκοφα (ἐκ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.37, (περι-) 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.42, (συγ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 169b}}; _Epic dialect_ _participle_ κεκοπώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.60 (variant -φώς, -πών), 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.335}}:—_middle_, _future_ κόψομαι {{LXX|LXX.Eze.6.9}}: _aorist_ ἐκοψάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.166:}} —_passive_, _future_ κεκόψομαι (ἀπο-) {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1125, (ἐκ-) [prev. author] “Ra.” 1223, (κατα-) 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.16, κοπήσομαι LXX.Jer.8.2, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.759}}: _aorist_ ἐκόπην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1278, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 723, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.13}}: _perfect_ κέκομμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 683}}:— **cut, strike**,
**1** **smite**, ο᾽ ἀμφὶ κάρη κεκοπὼς χερσὶ στιβαρῇσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.335: with double _accusative_, κόψε δὲ παπτήναντα παρήϊον}} **smote** him **on** the cheek, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.690.}}
**2** **smite** with weapons, κόπτοντες δούρεσσι μετάφρενον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.528; τοῖσι Πέρσῃσι εἵποντο κόπτοντες 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.113}}: _metaphorically_ in _passive_, with play on words, αἰεὶ κόπτῃ ῥήμασι καὶ κοπίσιν {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.335.}}
**3** **smite, slaughter** an animal with an axe or mallet, κόψας ἐξόπιθεν κεράων βοός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.521, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.425, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.6; in Trag., 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1278, “Eu.” 635, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 838.}}
**4** **cut off, chop off**, κεφαλὴν ἀπὸ δειρῆς κόψεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.203; χεῖράς τ᾽ ἠδὲ πόδας κόπτον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.477; κ. [τὰ γέρρα] ταῖς μαχαίραις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.26}}; κ. δένδρα **cut down** or **fell** trees, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.75, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.39, 43}}; κ. τὴν χώραν **lay** it **waste**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.2.26, 4.6.5}}:—in _passive_, of ships, **to be shattered, disabled** by the enemy, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.14, 8.13:— _metaphorically_, φρενῶν κεκομμένος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 479 (Lyric poetry)}}; τὸν ὕπνον ἁ φροντὶς κόπτοισα **preventing**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.28}}; [πνεῦμα] κοπτόμενον **being suddenly stopped, arrested**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 367a10.}}
**5** **strike, beat** a horse, to make him go faster, κόψε δ᾽ Ὀδυσσεὺς τόξῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.513}}; also σκηπανίῳ Γαιήοχος ἀμφοτέρω (i.e. Αἴαντε) κεκοπὼς πλῆσεν μένεος {{Refs|13.60.}}
**6** **hammer, forge**, κόπτε δὲ δεσμούς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|18.379, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.274}}; later, **stamp** metal, i.e. **coin** money, κ. νόμισμα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 12(5).480.11 (Siphnos, Athenian Law), 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 4, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.56}}:—_middle_, **coin oneself** money, **order to be coined**, κ. χρυσοῦ καὶ ἀργύρου νόμισμα {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.94, compare 4.166}}:—_passive_, of money, **to be stamped** or **coined**, [νομίσμασιν] μόνοις ὀρθῶς κοπεῖσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 723, compare 726.}}
**7** **knock** or **rap at**, τὴν θύραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 132, “Pl.” 1097, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.41, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.7, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 538, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.30 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 8, etc.}}; without θύραν, οὗτος, τί κόπτει; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 976.}}
**8** **pound, bray** in a mortar, κυπἐρου κεκομμένου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.71}}; ἀσταφίδα κεκ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 127.4}}; ἔλαιον κεκ., i.e. **pure** oil, {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.5.11.}}
**9** **knock, dash about**, τὸ ὕδωρ ὅταν κοπῇ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 60b; κόνις.. κοπτομένη.. ὑφ᾽ ἅρμασι 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 63; θάλασσα κοπτομένη πνοιαῖς 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.16.}}
**10** of birds, **peck**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 609b5}}; ὁ ἁλιάετος.. τὰ λιμναῖα κ. **preys** on the lagoon life, {{Refs|[prev. work] 593b24}}; σπειρὴν κ. **peck** at, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 449}}; of fish, **gnaw**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 620b17}}; of a snake, **strike**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.204}}:—_passive_, of wood or seeds, **to be worm-eaten**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.18.5, 8.11.2.}}
**10.b** **munch, masticate**, uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Chionides Comicus 6.}}
**11** ὁ ἵππος κ. τὸν ἀναβάτην **jars** his rider **by his paces**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 1.4}}:—_passive_,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 8.7, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 21.}}
**12** κ. ὄνους **dress, prepare** mill-stones for use, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 13}}; **set, sharpen**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.84}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.253 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus).}}
**13** _metaphorically_, **tire out, weary**, μήθ᾽ ὑμῖν ἐνοχλῶ μήτ᾽ ἐμαυτὸν κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 29, compare 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 2.3; λέγων φαίνου τι δὴ καινὸν.., ἢ μὴ κόπτε με Hegesippus Epigrammaticus 1.3, compare 3rd c.BC(?): Sosipater Comicus 1.20; μὴ κόπτ᾽ ἔμ᾽, ἀλλὰ τὰ κρέα 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 173.12; κ. τὴν ἀκρόασιν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 19; κ. τὰ ὦτα 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 6.119; κ. ἐρωτήμασιν ἀκαίροις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 7, cf. Moer. p.74}} P.:—_passive_, **to be worn out**, κοπτόμενοι ἀεὶ ταῖς στρατείαις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.16}}.
**II** _middle_ κόπτομαι, **beat** or **strike oneself, beat one's breast** or **head** through grief, κεφαλὴν δ᾽ ὅ γε κόψατο χερσίν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.33, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.δ (also _active_ τί κόπτεις τὴν κεφαλή; 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἥρως” 4); κόπτεσθαι μέτωπα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.58 (with μαχαίρῃσι added 2.61): _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 60b, “R.” 619c}}: _perfect_ _passive_, [πόλις] κέκοπται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 683}}:—_active_ with _accusative_ _cognate_, ἐκοψα κομμὸν Ἄριον {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ch.” 423 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** κόπτεσθαί τινα **mourn for** any one, κόπτεσθ᾽ Ἄδωνιν [{{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 396, cf. NT.Luke.8.52; but also ἐπί τινα NT.Rev.1.7, 18.9 (variant{αὐτῇ})}}]. (Cf. Lithuanian _kapóti_, Lett. **kapāt** 'chop small', 'beat', 'stamp', Latin _capo_ 'capon', perhaps σκέπαρνον.)

2876:
** κόραξ**, ᾰκος, ὁ,
**raven, Corvus corax** (not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus)}}; πάντα τάδ᾽ ἐν κοράκεσσι καὶ ἐν φθόρῳ 'food for **crows**', {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 833; κόρακες ὣς ἄκραντα γαρύετον Διὸς πρὸς ὄρνιχα θεῖον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.87; ἐπὶ σώματος δίκαν κόρακος.. σταθεῖσα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1473 (Lyric poetry); κόρακες ὥστε βωμῶν ἀλέγοντες οὐδέν [prev. author] “Supp.” 751 (Lyric poetry); κόραξι καὶ λύκοις χαρίζεσθαι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 8}}; in imprecations, ἐς κόρακας 'go and be hanged', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 852, 982; βάλλ᾽ ἐς κ. [prev. author] “Nu.” 133; ἀπόφερ᾽ ἐς κ. [prev. author] “Pax” 1221}}; οὐκ ἐς κ. ἐρρήσετε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 500; ἔρρ᾽ ἐς κ. 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 70; πλείτω ἐς κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1314}}; οὐκ ἐς κ. ἀποφθερε; {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 789; ἐς κ. οἰχήσεται [prev. author] “V.” 51; ἐξελῶ σ᾽ ἐς κ. ἐκ τῆς οἰκίας [prev. author] “Nu.” 123; ἐς κ. ἔρρειν ἐκ τῆς Ἀττικῆς 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 94.5}}: as a prophet of bad weather, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 253, Thphr “Sign.” 16, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.129a, etc.}}; of fair weather, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 1003, “Geoponica” 1.2.6, etc.}}; λευκὸς κ., proverbial of something unheard of, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.417, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “epigram” 43; but white ravens are mentioned by 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 519a6.}}
**2** **cormorant, Phalacrocorax carbo**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 593b18.}}
**3** the constellation {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Corvus”, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 449, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 27, etc.}}
**4** title of a grade in the mysteries of Mithras, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 4.16.}}
**II** **anything hooked** or **pointed like a raven's beak**,{{Refs|compare κορώνη 11}}
**II.1** **engine for grappling ships**,{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Plb 1.22.3, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.106.}}
**II.1.b** **siege-engine**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 100.18, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.44.}}
**II.2** **hooked door-handle**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 7, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.203, 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Sophisticos Elenchos commentarium - pars” 25.17}}; **hook** on a machine, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Automatopoetica” 15.3, 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 49.4.16, 2nd c.BC: Athenaeus Mechanicus 36.10, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Bito Mechanicus 50.9}}: generally, **hook,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|Sammelb. 1.24 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.3** **instrument of torture**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nec.” 11 (assuming variant).}}
**II.4** ={κατακλείς}, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 79.11.}}
**II.5** **point** of a surgical knife, σμιλαρίου {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 44.10.5}}; κατιάδος {{Refs 4th c.AD+|[prev. author] cited in Scholia 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 44.14.4.}}
**II.6** **cock's bill**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**III** **tub-fish** (compare κορακῖνος), {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Diphilus Siphnius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.356a.}}
**IV** a plaster, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 5.127, 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “fragment” 84. (Cf. κορώνη}}, Latin _corvus_, **cornix**, etc.)

2885:
** κοσμ-έω**,
**order, arrange**, especially **set** an army **in array, marshal** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.379; κοσμῆσαι ἵππους τε καὶ ἀνέρας 2.554}}:—_passive_, ἐπεὶ κόσμηθεν ἅμ᾽ ἡγεμόνεσσιν ἕκαστοι {{Refs|3.1}}; πένταχα κοσμηθέντες **marshalled** in five bodies, {{Refs|12.87}}; of a population, διὰ τρίχα κοσμηθέντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.655; once in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, of hunters, διὰ δὲ τρίχα κοσμηθέντες 9.157:}} —_middle_, κοσμησάμενος πολιήτας **having arranged his** men, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.806}}; so after Homer, κ. στρατόν (variant for{κοιμήσων}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 662; τάξεις κεκοσμημέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.26, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 247a; ἐπὶ τάξις πλεῦνας ἐκεκοσμέατο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.31.}}
**2** generally, **arrange, prepare**, δόρπον ἐκόσμει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.13; κ. ἀοιδήν η “Bacch.” 59; ἔργα 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 306; στέφανον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 74; τράπεζαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.6; εἰς τάφον λέβητα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1401}}:—_passive_, δεῖπνον κεκόσμηται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.22}}; δεῖ οὕτω κοσμηθῆναι ὅκως.. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 266}}; τὸ κοσμηθὲν αἷμα, ={τὸνοἰκεῖον κόσμον κτησάμενον}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.551.}}
**II** **order, rule**, τὴν πόλιν κ. καλῶς τε καὶ εὖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1103; Σπάρτην ἔλαχες, κείνην κόσμει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 723 (_anapaest meter_)}}; κ. ἐμαυτόν **restrain** myself, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hyps.fragment” 34(60).46; τὰ ἄλλα ἐκεκοσμέατό οἱ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.100; τόν γε νοῦν κοσμοῦντα πάντα κοσμεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 97 c:}} —_passive_, τὰ κοσμούμενα **orderly institutions**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 677}}: _perfect_ _participle_, of persons, **orderly**, ταπεινὸς καὶ κεκοσμημένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 716a; τεταγμένον τε καὶ κ. πρᾶγμα [prev. author] “Grg.” 504 a.}}
**II.2** in Crete, **hold office of** κόσμος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|111, οἱ κεκοσμηκότες 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1272a35, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 22.15.1}}; Cretan dialect **κοσμίω** {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 1.51, etc.}}; also κορμίω (which see).
**III** **adorn, equip, dress**, especially of women, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Hymni Homerici” 6.11, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 72; κοσμῆσαί τινα πανοπλίῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.180; τριπόδεσσι κ. δόμον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.19; τινὰ πλούτῳ ὑπερβάλλοντι 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 3.10.6: with double _accusative_, πρίν σε νυμφικὸν ἰστέφανον κοσμήσαμεν “JRS” 17.51 (Phrygia, 4th c.AD):—frequently in}}_middle_, κοσμέεσθαι τὰς κεφαλάς **to adorn their** heads, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.209; κοσμεῖσθαι σῶμα ὅπλοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1359}}; ἐν φοινικίσι κοσμησάμενοι having decked themselves who roasts {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 208}}:—_passive_, χρυσῷ κοσμηθεῖσα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Venerem” 65; παῖσα δ᾽ Ἄρῃ κεκόσμηται στέγα 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 15.1; ἵπποι κεκοσμημένοι ὡς κάλλιστα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.40; κεκ. ἐσθῆτι ποικίλῃ καὶ χρυσοῖσι στεφάνοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 535 d, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1064, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.41, etc.}}
**III.2** _metaphorically_, **adorn, embellish**, λόγους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 576; λόγους ῥήμασί τε καὶ ὀνόμασι κεκοσμημένους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 17 c; τραγικὸν λῆρον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1005}}; κ. ἔργον ἄριστον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1027; τὸ λογικὸν ἔχεις ἐξαίρετον, τοῦτο κόσμει 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.1.26; λόγον εὐρυθμίαις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.27; αὑτὸν λόγοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 196 b, compare 197 c; ἐπὶ τὸ μεῖζον κ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.21}}; τὸν.. τὴν ἐκείνων ἀρετὴν κοσμήσοντα (in speaking) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.287}}:—_passive_, ἦθος σεμνότητι -μημένον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.52 M.}}
**III.3** **honour**, λουτροῖς σ᾽ ἐκόσμης᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1139; κ. τάφον [prev. author] “Ant.” 396; νέκυν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1147; κ. καὶ τιμᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.3}}; of persons, **adorn, be an honour to**, πατρίδα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 947; νᾶσον εὐκλέα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.46; Σαλαμῖνα κ. πατρίδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 530.3; [τὴν πόλιν] αἱ τῶνδε ἀρεταὶ ἐκόσμησαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.42.}}
**III.4** **bury**, {{Refs|“JHS” 25.172, +others (from Isauria).}}
**IV** _passive_, **to be assigned, ascribed to**, ἐς τὸν Αἰγύπτιον νομὸν αὗται [αἱ πόλεις] ἐκεκοσμέατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.91; ἐς Πέρσας κεκοσμέαται [prev. author] 6.41}}; especially of philosophic schools, κατὰ τὴν Ἀκαδημίαν κοσμεῖσθαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.231; οἱ κατὰ διαφόρους αἱρέσεις κοσμούμενοι [prev. author] “M.” 11.77.}}

2886:
** κοσμ-ικός**, ή, όν,
(κόσμος IV) **of the world** or **universe**, σχήματα {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in primum Euclidis librum commentarius” p.65}} F.; τὰ κ. πάντα variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 196a25, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus (?) 23; ἡ κ. διάταξις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.119 f; κ. ὀχλήσεις 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Par.” 11}}; κλίσεις (variant{κλήσεις}) [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see at {Ὀρφεύς}: Astrology texts, κ. κέντρα (opposed to γενεθλιαλογικά) 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 79.26}}]. _adverb_ - κῶς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 119.15, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 112.}}
**II** **of this world, earthly,** {{NT|NT.Heb.9.1}}; **worldly**, ἐπιθυμίαι {{NT|NT.Tit.2.12.}}
**II.2** **secular, lay**, opposed to clerical, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 123.1.2.}}

2887:
** κόσμ-ιος**, α, ον,
(-ος, ον, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.606, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.3)}}, **well-ordered, regular, moderate**, δαπάνη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 56o d; οἴκησις [prev. author] “Criti.” 112}} c; κόσμιόν ἐστι, with _infinitive_, is **a regular practice**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 565.}}
**2** of persons, **orderly, well-behaved**, δίκαιοι καὶ σοφοὶ καὶ κ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 89; κ. καὶ σώφρων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.19; κ. καὶ εὔκολοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 329 d; κ. καὶ φρόνιμος ψυχή [prev. author] “Phd.” 108 a; χρηστὸς εἶ καὶ κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Nicopho Comicus 16; ἥτις ἐστὶ κοσμία γυνή 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 56, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1277b23; κ. ἐν διαίτῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 408 b; πρὸς τοὺς θεούς [prev. author] “Smp.” 193 a; οἱ κοσμιώτατοι φύσει [prev. author] “R.” 564}} e; of a patient, **quiet**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 65}}: frequently in Oratt., of **honest, orderly** citizens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 26.3, etc.; τοὺς πολίτας -ιωτέρους ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 20.18}}; **modest**, ὁμιλία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.11.14 (_superlative_)}}; τὸ κ. **decency, order**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 872, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 802 e.}} _adverb_ κοσμίως **regularly, decently**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 709, 978, +others; κ. ἔχειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 68}} c; κ. ἥκομεν **as befits** us, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Sph.” 216 a; κ. βιοῦν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.6}}: _comparative_ -ιώτερον, βεβιωκέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.162}}: _superlative_ -ώτατα, τὰς συμφορὰς φέρειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.4}}.
**II** _substantive_ κόσμιος, ὁ, (κόσμος IV) ={κοσμοπολίτης}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.600 f, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.9.1.}}

2888:
** κοσμο-κράτωρ** [ᾰ], ορος, ὁ,
**lord of the world**, epithet of οὐρανός, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Hymni” 4.3; Ζεὺς Μίτρας Ἥλιος κ. “Not.Scav.” 1912.323 (from Rome).}}
**2** of the Emperors, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IG” 14.926, “Sammelb.” 4275, compare 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 175, 4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 1.1.}}
**3** Astrology texts, **ruler of the** κόσμος, i.e. **planet**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author]in “Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 6.68, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 171.6; οἱ ἑπτὰ κ. 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 131}}; οἱ κ. τοῦ σκότους τούτου the **cosmic rulers** of this sinful world, {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Eph.6.12; οἱ κ. οἱ τὰ ὑπὸ σελήνην στοιχεῖα διοικοῦντες 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 2.3.}}

2889:
** κόσμος**, ὁ,
**order**, κατὰ κόσμον in **order, duly**, εὖ κατὰ κ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.472, +others}}; οὐ κατὰ κ. **shamefully**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.179; μὰψ ἀτὰρ οὐ κατὰ κ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.214: frequently in _dative_, κόσμῳ καθίζειν}} to sit **in order**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.77, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.67; οὐ κ... ἐλευσόμεθα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.225; κ. θεῖναι τὰ πάντα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.52, compare 7.36, etc.; διάθες τάδε κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1331}}; κ. φέρειν bear **becomingly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.82; δέξασθαί τινα κ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 521; σὺν κόσμῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.86, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 398b23; ἐν κόσμῳ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” fragment 378, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Smp.” 223b}}; κόσμῳ οὐδενὶ κοσμηθέντες **in** no sort of **order**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.59}}; φεύγειν, ἀπιέναι οὐδενὶ κ., {{Refs|[prev. author] 3.13, 8.60.γ}}, etc.; ἀτάκτως καὶ οὐδενὶ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.108, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 400}}; οὐκέτι τὸν αὐτὸν κ. no longer **in** the same **order**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.66; οὐδένα κ. [prev. work] 65, 69; ἦν δ᾽ οὐδεὶς κ. τῶν ποιουμένων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.77}}: generally, of things, **natural order**, γίνεται τῶν τεταρταίων ἡ κατάστασις ἐκ τούτου τοῦ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 20.}}
**2** **good order, good behaviour ** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 901}}; κ. χείρ, of a beggar, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.212 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus); κ. ἱστίον 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.107}}; κοῖλος μήν **short** month, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 8.3, compare κοιλοποιέομαι, κοῖλος 11.3: _comparative_ 492 ότερος ὁλμοῦ 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.52}}; ἐξηγεομένων.. τὸν κ. αὐτοῦ the **fashion** of it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.22}}; κ. τόνδε.. ὁ καταστησάμενος who established this **order** or **from**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.99.}}
**4** of states, **order, government**, μεταστῆσαι τὸν κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.76, compare 8.48, 67; μένειν ἐν τῷ ὀλιγαρχικῷ κ. 8.72, etc.}}; especially of the Spartan **constitution**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.65, 4th-3rd c.BC: Clearchus Historicus 3: _plural_, πόλεων κόσμοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 322c.}}
**II** **ornament, decoration**, especially of women, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.187, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 76, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.92. ; γυναικεῖος κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 373c, etc.}}; of a horse, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.145}}; of men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.123, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 397, etc.}}; γλαυκόχροα κόσμον ἐλαίας, of an olive-wreath, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.13, compare 8.83, “P.” 2.10, etc.; κ. κυνῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 6.1; κ. καὶ ἔπιπλα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.19}}; κ. ἀργυροῦς a **service** of plate, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 6.231b; ἱερὸς κ. “OGI” 90.40 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC)}}: _plural_, **ornaments**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1271; οἱ περὶ τὸ σῶμα κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.32}}: _metaphorically_, of ornaments of speech, such as **epithets**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.9 (_plural_), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1408a14, “Po.” 1457b2, 1458a33}}; ἁδυμελῆ κ. κελαδεῖν to sing sweet songs of **praise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 11 (10). b2.595, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.60.6.}}
**II.4** κ. νοσήματα **internal** complaints, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.44. ει τινί}} it does one **credit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.60, 142; γύναι, γυναιξὶ κόσμον ἡ σιγὴ φέρει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 293; κ. τοῦτ᾽ ἐστὶν ἐμοί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 914; οἷς κόσμος [ἐστὶ] καλῶς τοῦτο δρᾶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.5; ἐν κόσμῳ καὶ τιμῇ εἶναί τινι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.36}}; of persons, σὺ ἔμοιγε μέγιστος κ. ἔσει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.4.3; ἡ μεγαλοψυχία οἷον κ. τις τῶν ἀρετῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1124a1.}}
**III** **ruler, regulator**, title of chief magistrate in Crete, {{Refs|“SIG” 712.57, etc.}}; collectively, **body of** κόσμοι, {{Refs|[prev. work]524.1}}; τοῦ κ. τοῖς πλίασι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work]527.74: also frequently in _plural_, [prev. work]528.1, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1272a6, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.4.18, 22}}; compare κόρμος.
**IV** Philos., **world-order, universe**, first in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus, _accusative_ to “Placita Philosophorum” 2.1.1, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 8.48 (compare [5th-6th c.BC: Philolaus Philosophus] 21), or 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus, _accusative_ to 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus cited in D.L.[same place]; κόσμον τόνδε οὔτε τις θεῶν οὔτε ἀνθρώπων ἐποίησεν, ἀλλ᾽ ἦν ἀεὶ καὶ ἔστιν καὶ ἔσται πῦρ 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 30; ὁ καλούμενος ὑπὸ τῶν σοφιστῶν κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.11: frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, “Grg.” 508a, “Ti.” 27a, +others; ἡ τοῦ ὅλου σύστασίς ἐστι κ. καὶ οὐρανός 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 280a21, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.37U., 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.168, etc.; ὁ κ. ζῷον ἔμψυχον καὶ λογικόν 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.139, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 30b}}: sometimes of the **firmament**, γῆς ἁπάσης τῆς ὑπὸ τῷ κόσμῳ κειμένης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.179; ὁ περὶ τὴν γῆν ὅλος κ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 339a20}}; μετελθεῖν εἰς τὸν ἀέναον κ., of death, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 56.48 (Canopus, 3rd c.BC)}}; but also, of **earth**, as opposed to heaven, ὁ ἐπιχθόνιος κ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.49.44; or as opposed to the underworld, ὁ ἄνω κ. 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 27.123}}; of any **region** of the universe, ὁ μετάρσιος κ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 1.49.44}}; of the sphere whose centre is the earth's centre and radius the straight line joining earth and sun, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “ψαμμίτης” 4}}; of the sphere containing the fixed stars, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 987b}}: in _plural_, **worlds**, coexistent or successive, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anaximander Philosophus et alii cited in “Placita Philosophorum” 2.1.3, cf. Epicur.[same place]}}; also, of stars, Νὺξ μεγάλων κ. κτεάτειρα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 356 (_anapaest meter_), cf. Heraclid.et Pythagorei cited in “Placita Philosophorum” 2.13.15 (= 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 22)}}; οἱ ἑπτὰ κ. the Seven **planets,** {{Refs|Corp.Herm. 11.7.}}
**IV.2** _metaphorically_, **microcosm**, ἄνθρωπος μικρὸς κ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 34; ἄνθρωπος βραχὺς κ. 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.155}}; of living beings in general, τὸ ζῷον οἷον μικρόν τινα κ. εἶναί φασιν ἄνδρες παλαιοί {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 3.10.}}
**IV.3** in later Gr., ={οἰκουμένη}, **the known** or **inhabited world**, {{NT+1st c.BC+|“OGI” 458.40 (1st c.BC), NT.Rom.1.8, etc.}}; ὁ τοῦ παντὸς κ. κύριος, of Nero, {{Refs|“SIG” 814.31, compare “IGRom.” 4.982 (from Samos)}}; ἐὰν τὸν κ. ὅλον κερδήσῃ {{NT|NT.Matt.16.26.}}
**IV.4** **men in general**, φανέρωσον σεαυτὸν τῷ κ. {{NT|NT.John.7.4, compare 12.19}}; especially of the **world** as estranged from God by sin, {{NT|[prev. work] 16.20, 17.9, +others, NT.1Cor.1.21, etc.}}
**IV.5** οὗτος ὁ κ. this present **world**, i.e. **earth**, opposed to heaven, {{NT|NT.John.13.1; regarded as the kingdom of evil, ὁ ἄρχων τοῦ κ. τούτου [prev. work] 12.31.}}
**V** Pythag.name for **six,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 37}}; for **ten**, {{Refs|[prev. work]59.}}

2893:
** κουφ-ίζω**,
_Attic dialect_ _future_ - ῐῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1287}}: _perfect_ κεκούφικα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.12 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC):}}
**I** **to be light**, κουφίζουσαν ἄρουραν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 463, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1555}}; of a sufferer, **to be relieved**, κουφίζειν δοκῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 735, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.27.}}
**II** _transitive_, **lighten, make light**, τὸ κενὸν ἐμπεριλαμβανόμενον κ. τὰ σώματα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 309a6}}:—_passive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “PA” 663b13: hence}}
**II.1** **lift up, raise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 43, “Tr.” 1025 (Lyric poetry); αἴρων κουφιῶ σ᾽ ἐγώ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1762 (Lyric poetry); ἀσπίδ᾽ ἀμφὶ βραχίονι κουφίζων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 121 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἅλμα κουφιεῖν **make a light** leap, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Aj.” [same place]; κ. πήδημα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 861 (Lyric poetry)}}; δύστηνον αἰώρημα κουφίζω, ={δύστηνος αἰωροῦμαι}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Supp.” 1047}}:—_passive_, **to be lifted up, soar**, [τῷ πτερῷ] ᾧ ψυχὴ κουφίζεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 248c, compare 249a; σώματα -όμενα ὑπὸ τοῦ κύματος 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.27c.}}
**II.2** **lighten of** a load, ὄχλου πλήθους τε κ. χθόνα **lighten** earth **of** a multitude, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 40; κουφισθεὶς τοῦ βάρους 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.16.2}}: _absolutely_, **lighten** ships **of their cargo**, τῷ ταχυναυτοῦντι κουφίσαντες προσβάλλειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34; κουφισθεισῶν τῶν νεῶν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 20.5.11, compare 1.60.8.}}
**II.2.b** of persons, **relieve** from burdens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.1, “Cyr.” 6.3.24; τὸν δῆμον τῶν εἰσφορῶν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.64, compare “IG” 12(7).506.16 (Amorgos, 3rd c.BC)}}; τόκων τοὺς χρεωφειλέτας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 37}}; **relieve** (contractors), {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.17.5; τῆς ὑπερηφανίας 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.16 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; κ. τοὺς νοσοῦντας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1106c}}:—_passive_, **to be relieved**, ὅταν σῶμα κουφισθῇ νόσου **from**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 43; τοῦ πάθους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 873b22; λέξασα κουφισθήσομαι ψυχήν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 473:}}_future_ _middle_ κουφιεῖσθαι{{Refs 1st c.AD+|in _passive_ sense, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.145 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus: _metaphorically_, τῇ τῶνδε εὐκλείᾳ κουφίζεσθε}} **feel your burdens lightened** by.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.44; κουφίζονται οἱ λυπούμενοι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1171a29, compare “Pol.” 1342a14; ἐλπίδι κ. ματαίᾳ 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 11.33.}}
**II.3** with _accusative_ _of things_, **lighten, assuage**, ἀλγηδόνας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 573; συμφορὰς λόγῳ κ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.35; κ. ἔρωτα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 23.9; τὸ πάθος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 52; τὰ ὀφλήματα [prev. author] 2.807d}}: _absolutely_, **give** or **procure relief**, κ. οὐδέν, ἀλλά.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.7, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 725b9}}:—_passive_, νομίζοντες κεκουφίσθαι τὸν πόλεμον αὐτοῖς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.17.2.}}
**II.3.b** **cancel** a debt, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“POxy.” 126.8 (6th c.AD)}}, etc.:—_middle_, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PMasp.” 95.10 (6th c.AD).}}
**II.4** ἑαυτοὺς κ. **cheapen** themselves, uncertain in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 112 G.}}
**II.5** **subtract**, ἀπὸ τῶν μοιρῶν {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 2.1.}}

2894:
** κόφῐν-ος**, ὁ,
**basket**, _accusative_ to {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anecdota Graeca - Bekker” 102 less _Attic dialect_ than ἄρριχος, found in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1310, “fragment” 349, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 41, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.8.6, “IG” 22.1672.65, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 4.11, “PPetr.” 3p.312 (3rd c.BC)}}; in later times used specially by Jews, {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Juvenalis, D. Junius Poeta Latinus 3.14, 6.542, cf. NT.Matt.16.9.}}
**II** Boeotian measure, containing nine Attic choenices, i.e. about two gallons, κ. σίτου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 7.2712.65, compare 5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 13, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 629a13, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

2896:
** κράζω**,
not common in _present_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 287, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 609b24, “Po.” 1458 b31, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 52, “POxy.” 717 (1st c.BC)}}, etc.: _future_ κεκράξομαι {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 1, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Eq.” 285, 487, “Ra.” 258, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 204, later κράξω “Anthologia Graeca” 11.141 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus), NT.Luke.19.40}}: _aorist 1_ ἔκραξα {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 53, LXX.Josh.1.14, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.211 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus); _imperative_ κρᾶξον [ᾱ by nature] Hdn.Gr. 2.14}}; ἐκέκραξα frequently in {{LXX|LXX, LXX.Num.11.2, +others:}}_aorist 2_ ἔκρᾰγον (ἀν{{LXX+5th c.BC+|-) 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 428, etc., ἐκέκραγον LXX.Isa.6.4 (unless _imperfect_ of Κεκράγω): frequently in}}_perfect_ with _present_ sense, κέκρᾱγα (see. below) (late κέκρᾰγα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.86 (2nd-3rd c.AD: Rufinus Epigrammaticus)); _imperative_ κέκραχθι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 335, “V.” 198, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 235; _plural_ κεκράγετε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 415}}: _pluperfect_ ἐκεκράγειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eq.” 674, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.10:—post- 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **croak**, of the raven, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 208}}, Thphr. [prev. cited]; of frogs, κεκραξόμεσθα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ra.” [same place], compare 265}}: generally, **scream, shriek, cry**, σὺ δ᾽ αὖ κέκραγας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 743; παιδίον κεκραγός 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 11, 24}}; **bawl, shout**, κεκραγὼς καὶ βοῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 722, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.132}}; κεκραγέναι πρός τινα **to call to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 982; κραγὸν κεκράξεται [prev. author] “Eq.” 487 (compare κραγός)}}: with _accusative_ _cognate_, μέλος κέκραγα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 281.5}}; ποίου (i.e. περὶ ποίου) κέκραγας ἀνδρὸς ὧδ᾽ ὑπέρφρον; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1236}}:—rare in early Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus [same place], D.[same place], compare “POxy.” 717.1 (1st c.BC)}}, etc.; ἐκεκράγει ὅτι.. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 6.274f}}; κεκράγασιν ὡς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.108 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]2.98 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**2** with _accusative_ _of things_, **call, clamour for**, ἐμβάδας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 103.}}

2897:
** κραιπᾰλ-η**, ἡ,
**drinking-bout**, ἐκ κραιπάλης {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 277, “V.” 1255; τὰς κεφαλὰς ὑγιεῖς ἔχειν ἐκ κ. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 9.8; κ. καὶ μέθη NT.Luke.21.34; χθεσινὴ κ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Laps.” 1, compare 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Bello Gothico” 1.3.}}
**2** **intoxication**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Κόλαξ” 47, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.37, 4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Orationes” 2.36a.}}
**3** **drunken headache**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 3, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 873a37, etc.}}

2898:
** κρᾱνίον**, τό,
(κάρα) **upper part of the head, skull** (κεφαλῆς τὸ τριχωτὸν μέρος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 491a31, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.739)}}; of horses, ὅθι τε πρῶται τρίχες ἵππων κρανίῳ ἐμπεφύασι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.84}}; of men, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).54, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 683, 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 71, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 299e, etc.}}: generally, **head**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 16.}}
**II** **headache,** [{{Refs|Hippiatr. 103 (variant{κατακράνιον}).}}]

2899:
** κράσπεδ-ον**, τό,
**edge, border, skirt**, especially of cloth, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.53}}; of the **fringe** or **tassel** worn by Jews, {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Matt.9.20: mostly in _plural_, ἄκροισι λαίφους κρασπέδοις (see. ἄκρος 1.2b) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 524; κράσπεδα στεμμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 475, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 43.30; χρυσᾶ κ. Chamaeleon Grammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 9.374a, 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.36, 37.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, mostly in _plural_, **skirts** or **edge** of a country, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 602, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 381}}; of a mountain, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.6.8}}; πρὸς κρασπέδοισι στρατοπέδου on **the skirts** of the army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 661; τοὺς πελταστὰς ἐπὶ τὰ κ. ἑκατέρωθεν καθίστασθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.16}}: also in _singular_, Τιμολέοντα ὥσπερ ἐκ κ. τινὸς λεπτοῦ τῆς πολίχνης τῇ Σικελίᾳ προσηρτημένον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 11; κ. αἰγιαλοῦ “Anthologia Graeca” 7.78 (Dionysius Epigrammaticus Cyzic.).}}
**3** in Medicine texts, **affection of the uvula, fimbria**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.8.}}
